1
00:00:01,547 --> 00:00:03,517
(The Man Has Come)

2
00:00:04,087 --> 00:00:06,787
- Nice to see you, everyone. - Nice to see you.

3
00:00:06,957 --> 00:00:09,286
- It's been a while, Kai. - Nice to see you all.

4
00:00:09,287 --> 00:00:11,426
- It's great to be back. - My gosh. Yes.

5
00:00:11,427 --> 00:00:13,296
You're here for the man of the hour today.

6
00:00:13,297 --> 00:00:15,727
That's right. I've always wanted to meet him.

7
00:00:16,097 --> 00:00:19,466
I also wanted to congratulate him from the bottom of my heart.

8
00:00:19,467 --> 00:00:21,607
- Congratulate him? - It's the most prestigious...

9
00:00:21,707 --> 00:00:24,067
awards ceremony in the American theater and musical world.

10
00:00:24,437 --> 00:00:25,536
It's none other than the Tony Awards ceremony.

11
00:00:25,537 --> 00:00:26,636
- Tony? - Tony Awards.

12
00:00:26,637 --> 00:00:28,147
His musical won six awards at the ceremony.

13
00:00:28,377 --> 00:00:30,246
He's the first Korean writer to receive such honors.

14
00:00:30,247 --> 00:00:33,347
His musical is sold out for every showing on Broadway...

15
00:00:33,517 --> 00:00:35,617
even as we speak.

16
00:00:35,747 --> 00:00:37,286
He's the writer of the musical, "Maybe Happy Ending."

17
00:00:37,287 --> 00:00:39,056
I'd like to invite in Hue Park. Please come on out!

18
00:00:39,057 --> 00:00:42,686
(Welcome, New Member Hue Park.)

19
00:00:42,687 --> 00:00:44,287
- Hello. - It's Hue Park.

20
00:00:45,197 --> 00:00:48,767
("Maybe Happy Ending")

21
00:00:49,127 --> 00:00:51,167
("Il Tenore")

22
00:00:51,267 --> 00:00:52,797
("Ghost Bakery")

23
00:00:53,167 --> 00:00:55,607
(He's been writing prominent musicals since 2012.)

24
00:00:56,467 --> 00:00:58,236
(The world-class creator)

25
00:00:58,237 --> 00:00:59,977
(Hue Park)

26
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:01,906
- Hello. - Hello.

27
00:01:01,907 --> 00:01:04,007
- Nice to meet you. - My gosh.

28
00:01:04,077 --> 00:01:05,116
All right.

29
00:01:05,117 --> 00:01:06,617
Please say hello to our viewers.

30
00:01:06,917 --> 00:01:09,286
Hello, I'm a musical writer,

31
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:11,146
a lyricist, and a director.

32
00:01:11,147 --> 00:01:13,517
My name is Hue Park. Nice to meet you.

33
00:01:14,017 --> 00:01:15,326
- Nice to meet you. - Hue Park.

34
00:01:15,327 --> 00:01:17,287
- We call them the EGOT. - Right.

35
00:01:17,387 --> 00:01:18,827
- A Korean singer won a Grammy. - Yes.

36
00:01:19,027 --> 00:01:20,196
- Right. - Jo Sumi won the award.

37
00:01:20,197 --> 00:01:21,927
- That's right. - And the Emmy Award too.

38
00:01:21,997 --> 00:01:23,166
Director Hwang Dong Hyuk of "Squid Game."

39
00:01:23,167 --> 00:01:25,196
- That's right. - Bong Joon Ho won an Oscar.

40
00:01:25,197 --> 00:01:26,436
- Right. - With "Parasite."

41
00:01:26,437 --> 00:01:27,467
- He won it with "Parasite." - Yes.

42
00:01:27,967 --> 00:01:29,267
No Korean has ever won a Tony Award.

43
00:01:29,807 --> 00:01:31,776
Thanks to your Tony Award,

44
00:01:31,777 --> 00:01:34,007
Korea became the only non-English speaking country that...

45
00:01:34,177 --> 00:01:36,476
won an award from all four major performing arts awards.

46
00:01:36,477 --> 00:01:37,607
Gosh, really?

47
00:01:37,647 --> 00:01:39,216
Is it like Messi winning the World Cup?

48
00:01:39,217 --> 00:01:40,347
- Let's give it up for him. - Gosh.

49
00:01:40,847 --> 00:01:42,146
- Thank you. - Congratulations.

50
00:01:42,147 --> 00:01:43,716
- Gosh. - Congratulations.

51
00:01:43,717 --> 00:01:46,016
Is that why you two are dressed all formally?

52
00:01:46,017 --> 00:01:47,817
- We're copying his fashion style. - What?

53
00:01:48,227 --> 00:01:49,986
- Your outfit... - You look like a parent...

54
00:01:49,987 --> 00:01:51,756
- at your child's recital. - With your shirt and all...

55
00:01:51,757 --> 00:01:52,757
No, you look very stylish.

56
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:54,527
It looks like you're going to a ball.

57
00:01:54,697 --> 00:01:58,167
You remind me of a scene from "The Phantom of the Opera."

58
00:01:58,297 --> 00:02:00,337
- You remind me of that scene. - I wish not to talk to you guys.

59
00:02:00,437 --> 00:02:01,966
You wish not to talk to us?

60
00:02:01,967 --> 00:02:03,166
You wish not to talk to us?

61
00:02:03,167 --> 00:02:04,167
- He can't even articulate now. - Listen.

62
00:02:04,168 --> 00:02:05,307
He's embarrassing us.

63
00:02:06,037 --> 00:02:07,946
- Have you watched his musical? - He can't even speak now.

64
00:02:07,947 --> 00:02:09,346
I went to the premiere.

65
00:02:09,347 --> 00:02:10,446
- Really? - You did?

66
00:02:10,447 --> 00:02:11,846
- You did? - Yes.

67
00:02:11,847 --> 00:02:12,916
(Smiling shyly)

68
00:02:12,917 --> 00:02:15,186
You received such a distinguished award.

69
00:02:15,187 --> 00:02:16,346
- I'm sure... - Right.

70
00:02:16,347 --> 00:02:17,516
You must've gotten many offers to join other TV shows.

71
00:02:17,517 --> 00:02:20,017
Could you tell us why you chose our show?

72
00:02:20,217 --> 00:02:22,227
The day after receiving the Tony Award,

73
00:02:22,757 --> 00:02:24,156
I checked my Instagram page.

74
00:02:24,157 --> 00:02:25,927
I had already gotten a direct message.

75
00:02:25,997 --> 00:02:27,157
Immediately after the ceremony?

76
00:02:27,327 --> 00:02:28,866
- So we were the first one to ask? - Gosh.

77
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:30,096
- Gosh, they're so fast. - I checked them in order.

78
00:02:30,097 --> 00:02:31,636
- That's amazing. - I bet they pulled an all-nighter.

79
00:02:31,637 --> 00:02:32,866
Don't star on other shows.

80
00:02:32,867 --> 00:02:34,067
- Just star on ours. - Right.

81
00:02:35,007 --> 00:02:37,337
- We'll go to New York for you. - Of course.

82
00:02:38,577 --> 00:02:40,807
(The Greatest Showman)

83
00:02:41,507 --> 00:02:43,246
(Times Square in New York)

84
00:02:43,247 --> 00:02:44,307
New York.

85
00:02:46,817 --> 00:02:49,387
(I Live Alone presents...)

86
00:02:50,347 --> 00:02:52,687
(Hue Park, )

87
00:02:53,417 --> 00:02:56,987
(Jun Hyun Moo, Park Na Rae, Kian84, CODE KUNST, KEY, )

88
00:02:58,197 --> 00:03:00,427
(and Kai.)

89
00:03:01,367 --> 00:03:03,767
- It's like a scene from a movie. - His clip surely looks different.

90
00:03:12,407 --> 00:03:14,947
I've been living alone for 18 years now.

91
00:03:15,177 --> 00:03:16,877
I'm Writer Hue Park.

92
00:03:18,977 --> 00:03:21,647
I currently reside in New York, United States.

93
00:03:22,887 --> 00:03:24,187
(New York)

94
00:03:27,657 --> 00:03:29,387
- Yes. - Times Square.

95
00:03:30,487 --> 00:03:31,597
Broadway.

96
00:03:33,157 --> 00:03:36,227
(It's the mecca of plays and musicals.)

97
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:40,737
(It's a world-leading center of culture.)

98
00:03:42,307 --> 00:03:43,536
- Gosh, "KPop Demon Hunters." - "Soda Pop."

99
00:03:43,537 --> 00:03:44,607
My goodness.

100
00:03:45,277 --> 00:03:48,446
I used to live with my family when I was in Korea.

101
00:03:48,447 --> 00:03:50,207
Then I came to New York for my studies.

102
00:03:50,877 --> 00:03:52,216
It's almost been 18 years.

103
00:03:52,217 --> 00:03:55,316
I've lived here for almost half my life.

104
00:03:55,317 --> 00:03:58,757
I think New York is part of my identity now.

105
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:00,156
So you weren't young when you went abroad.

106
00:04:00,157 --> 00:04:02,387
Right. I never had an early education abroad either.

107
00:04:02,557 --> 00:04:06,126
I went to New York for my studies at the age of 25.

108
00:04:06,127 --> 00:04:08,367
I thought he was younger than me.

109
00:04:08,797 --> 00:04:10,826
But he's two years older than me.

110
00:04:10,827 --> 00:04:13,497
I'm 42 years old.

111
00:04:13,637 --> 00:04:15,667
- You're older than Na Rae? - Yes.

112
00:04:18,007 --> 00:04:19,077
Yes. What?

113
00:04:19,977 --> 00:04:22,306
She's so beautiful.

114
00:04:22,307 --> 00:04:23,747
- I was surprised when I walked in. - Well...

115
00:04:24,547 --> 00:04:26,347
(Glancing)

116
00:04:26,547 --> 00:04:28,086
Given your outfit, you look like the Tony Award winner.

117
00:04:28,087 --> 00:04:29,947
(Given your outfit, you look like the Tony Award winner.)

118
00:04:30,417 --> 00:04:32,387
I used to be a fan of Tony, the singer.

119
00:04:32,487 --> 00:04:33,487
- Tony An? - Yes.

120
00:04:33,488 --> 00:04:34,517
Okay.

121
00:04:35,187 --> 00:04:37,557
(Current time, 6pm)

122
00:04:38,727 --> 00:04:41,097
It's odd, but when I'm in New York, I find myself walking fast.

123
00:04:42,327 --> 00:04:43,627
That place looks familiar.

124
00:04:44,997 --> 00:04:46,867
- You represent New York, don't you? - That's right.

125
00:04:48,067 --> 00:04:49,936
I guess there are many Korean residents in New York.

126
00:04:49,937 --> 00:04:51,337
(There are many Korean residents in New York besides me.)

127
00:04:51,907 --> 00:04:54,107
(Smug)

128
00:04:55,277 --> 00:04:56,576
- You were there for a few days. - Right.

129
00:04:56,577 --> 00:04:57,577
- But... - Come on.

130
00:04:57,578 --> 00:04:58,977
He ran a marathon there. So it counts as residency.

131
00:04:59,177 --> 00:05:00,817
I think of it as my home.

132
00:05:01,987 --> 00:05:03,246
Him and his cultural toadyism.

133
00:05:03,247 --> 00:05:04,347
(He's Asian just like me.)

134
00:05:04,547 --> 00:05:06,387
- He's a New Yorker. That's cool. - Right.

135
00:05:06,787 --> 00:05:08,056
The city looks so fancy.

136
00:05:08,057 --> 00:05:09,587
How come the city looks different from when Kian84 was there?

137
00:05:10,927 --> 00:05:12,626
(It's obvious that he's a tourist.)

138
00:05:12,627 --> 00:05:14,327
- The city looks the same. - It does look the same.

139
00:05:15,927 --> 00:05:20,137
(The New Yorker only looks ahead while walking.)

140
00:05:21,397 --> 00:05:24,237
(Crowded)

141
00:05:24,777 --> 00:05:26,737
(Glancing)

142
00:05:27,177 --> 00:05:29,547
(He keeps on walking without stopping.)

143
00:05:30,807 --> 00:05:32,247
They're waiting for his musical.

144
00:05:32,377 --> 00:05:34,117
I guess he's going to work now.

145
00:05:34,747 --> 00:05:36,747
It's the musical I worked on as a writer.

146
00:05:36,947 --> 00:05:39,717
It's the theater where "Maybe Happy Ending" will be staged.

147
00:05:40,057 --> 00:05:41,086
"Hue Park."

148
00:05:41,087 --> 00:05:44,327
It's Belasco Theater located on Broadway.

149
00:05:44,457 --> 00:05:46,296
The building has been there for over a century.

150
00:05:46,297 --> 00:05:48,426
- This theater, Belasco Theater, - A century.

151
00:05:48,427 --> 00:05:50,027
Is a landmark of the city.

152
00:05:50,567 --> 00:05:53,166
(Amid the galaxy of many other Broadway musicals...)

153
00:05:53,167 --> 00:05:55,107
I want to watch all of them.

154
00:05:55,707 --> 00:05:57,937
(Standing with a presence)

155
00:05:59,007 --> 00:06:00,507
- Is that a line? - A line?

156
00:06:00,807 --> 00:06:03,077
That's 30 minutes before the show.

157
00:06:03,147 --> 00:06:06,346
The line is long enough to circle around a block.

158
00:06:06,347 --> 00:06:08,617
A lot of people line up in advance.

159
00:06:08,717 --> 00:06:10,487
That's why he seemed proud.

160
00:06:11,887 --> 00:06:13,987
(He walks past the audience waiting in line.)

161
00:06:14,757 --> 00:06:15,857
Hey, Brian.

162
00:06:16,287 --> 00:06:17,886
(He enters through the side gate.)

163
00:06:17,887 --> 00:06:19,257
Gosh, he's entering through the side gate.

164
00:06:19,897 --> 00:06:22,296
- That's cooler. - We call that the stage door.

165
00:06:22,297 --> 00:06:26,196
The crew and cast can only access this door.

166
00:06:26,197 --> 00:06:27,397
(Only authorized personnel can use this door.)

167
00:06:30,107 --> 00:06:31,636
I can feel the long history of this building.

168
00:06:31,637 --> 00:06:32,637
And it makes the theater...

169
00:06:32,638 --> 00:06:33,677
- more romantic. - That's right.

170
00:06:33,937 --> 00:06:35,046
- Hey. - How is it going?

171
00:06:35,047 --> 00:06:36,106
(He goes through the stage door.)

172
00:06:36,107 --> 00:06:38,676
There's backstage past the stage door.

173
00:06:38,677 --> 00:06:40,417
It's the back of the stage.

174
00:06:40,577 --> 00:06:43,487
The dressing rooms of the cast are all there.

175
00:06:43,717 --> 00:06:45,456
Gosh, this is so cool.

176
00:06:45,457 --> 00:06:47,516
And there's also a room...

177
00:06:47,517 --> 00:06:49,586
where stage directors announce their calls.

178
00:06:49,587 --> 00:06:51,927
- That's how it looks. - That's where they give signals.

179
00:06:52,257 --> 00:06:54,196
There's also an area...

180
00:06:54,197 --> 00:06:57,397
where creators like me and the crew could stay and watch.

181
00:06:58,367 --> 00:06:59,466
(He's right at the forefront of a Broadway show.)

182
00:06:59,467 --> 00:07:00,736
Welcome back.

183
00:07:00,737 --> 00:07:02,506
From what I've heard,

184
00:07:02,507 --> 00:07:05,936
writers don't come to every show.

185
00:07:05,937 --> 00:07:07,136
But is it different on Broadway?

186
00:07:07,137 --> 00:07:10,676
Everyone including the writers must be at the theater...

187
00:07:10,677 --> 00:07:12,146
until the musical enters a stable phase.

188
00:07:12,147 --> 00:07:15,247
When a new actor or actress joins, writers come to the theater,

189
00:07:15,447 --> 00:07:18,517
practice with them, and help stabilize the show.

190
00:07:18,587 --> 00:07:21,256
Then who decides if your show is entering a stable phase?

191
00:07:21,257 --> 00:07:22,526
It's more...

192
00:07:22,527 --> 00:07:24,027
- of an implicit understanding. - So it's like a rough evaluation?

193
00:07:24,557 --> 00:07:25,827
I wouldn't say, "rough."

194
00:07:27,497 --> 00:07:29,127
Do you think they wouldn't make an effort?

195
00:07:29,597 --> 00:07:30,867
(Embarrassed)

196
00:07:31,167 --> 00:07:33,667
- He looks so small before fame. - You think they'd wing it?

197
00:07:35,337 --> 00:07:36,866
- Good to see you. - Good to see you.

198
00:07:36,867 --> 00:07:38,736
- Yes. - Good to see you.

199
00:07:38,737 --> 00:07:39,836
Good to see you, Hue.

200
00:07:39,837 --> 00:07:40,977
(He gives his colleagues a hug as soon as he comes to work.)

201
00:07:41,977 --> 00:07:43,576
- He's giving everyone a hug. - Just like that?

202
00:07:43,577 --> 00:07:45,677
- My gosh. Hey, you. - I know.

203
00:07:46,317 --> 00:07:47,546
Don't make me emotional.

204
00:07:47,547 --> 00:07:48,647
(Choking up)

205
00:07:49,487 --> 00:07:50,846
We've been working together for almost a year.

206
00:07:50,847 --> 00:07:52,187
So when I see them,

207
00:07:52,717 --> 00:07:53,787
I feel like I'm home.

208
00:07:55,727 --> 00:07:58,497
(Hue goes off somewhere after greeting his colleagues.)

209
00:08:00,857 --> 00:08:02,896
The building looks so classic.

210
00:08:02,897 --> 00:08:04,027
(He follows the voices of the people.)

211
00:08:06,497 --> 00:08:08,196
(The theater is packed with the audience.)

212
00:08:08,197 --> 00:08:09,636
My gosh. It's a full house.

213
00:08:09,637 --> 00:08:12,136
Lately, our ticket sales have hit over 100 percent...

214
00:08:12,137 --> 00:08:14,006
which I'm grateful for.

215
00:08:14,007 --> 00:08:15,746
We even sold out all the standing-room-only tickets.

216
00:08:15,747 --> 00:08:18,547
Our sales have been at 103 percent from what I've heard.

217
00:08:19,047 --> 00:08:20,377
What do you mean by 103 percent?

218
00:08:21,217 --> 00:08:22,586
It's like standing on a train...

219
00:08:22,587 --> 00:08:23,946
- without a seat. - That's right.

220
00:08:23,947 --> 00:08:25,017
- You can do that? - I see.

221
00:08:25,317 --> 00:08:28,157
- How many seats are there? - About 1,000 seats.

222
00:08:29,227 --> 00:08:31,356
How much is one ticket?

223
00:08:31,357 --> 00:08:32,556
(If you don't mind me asking...)

224
00:08:32,557 --> 00:08:35,897
The most expensive seat costs 500 dollars.

225
00:08:36,297 --> 00:08:38,497
- That's 700,000 won. - That's expensive.

226
00:08:38,897 --> 00:08:42,236
But on average, our tickets cost about 200 dollars.

227
00:08:42,237 --> 00:08:44,537
So that's a little short of 300,000 won.

228
00:08:44,867 --> 00:08:45,936
That's for a regular seat?

229
00:08:45,937 --> 00:08:47,076
Is there a friends and family discount?

230
00:08:47,077 --> 00:08:48,607
There are no friends and family discounts.

231
00:08:49,547 --> 00:08:50,607
It's a cold business model.

232
00:08:50,747 --> 00:08:53,076
The ticket system for Broadway shows...

233
00:08:53,077 --> 00:08:54,376
- is different from ours. - How?

234
00:08:54,377 --> 00:08:57,446
As for popular shows like this one,

235
00:08:57,447 --> 00:08:59,617
- the ticket prices can go up. - There's an added premium?

236
00:08:59,987 --> 00:09:02,126
- Right. - There's no ceiling for the prices.

237
00:09:02,127 --> 00:09:04,556
Looking at the number of the sold tickets,

238
00:09:04,557 --> 00:09:06,697
the production can increase the prices.

239
00:09:06,927 --> 00:09:08,866
- So it's like a market price? - Yes, it is.

240
00:09:08,867 --> 00:09:10,296
That's right. It's based on the market.

241
00:09:10,297 --> 00:09:11,996
- It's a capitalistic society. - Of course.

242
00:09:11,997 --> 00:09:13,466
- It's the ultimate capitalism. - Gosh.

243
00:09:13,467 --> 00:09:15,037
If you want to watch it so badly, pay up.

244
00:09:16,467 --> 00:09:18,377
- Hi. - Hey, Hue.

245
00:09:18,777 --> 00:09:20,777
I'm going to be standing right here. Yes.

246
00:09:21,877 --> 00:09:24,107
All right. Perfect. Thanks.

247
00:09:24,807 --> 00:09:26,476
There weren't any seats left for me either.

248
00:09:26,477 --> 00:09:28,146
So I just watched it standing in the back.

249
00:09:28,147 --> 00:09:29,346
But that's cooler.

250
00:09:29,347 --> 00:09:32,516
It's called the Writer's Alley.

251
00:09:32,517 --> 00:09:36,486
- It's an alley for the writers. - Writer's Alley.

252
00:09:36,487 --> 00:09:38,456
Writers can watch the show...

253
00:09:38,457 --> 00:09:40,426
all the way in the back of the theater...

254
00:09:40,427 --> 00:09:42,066
while looking at the backs of people's heads.

255
00:09:42,067 --> 00:09:43,896
- You can check their reaction. - To watch their reaction.

256
00:09:43,897 --> 00:09:45,466
Yes. So it's a bit nerve-racking.

257
00:09:45,467 --> 00:09:47,897
I can clearly see their unadulterated reaction.

258
00:09:48,167 --> 00:09:50,636
- Right. - It's like immediate feedback.

259
00:09:50,637 --> 00:09:52,177
- Yes. That's right. - Right.

260
00:09:52,907 --> 00:09:55,177
- You must be nervous. - That's a nerve-racking moment.

261
00:09:55,547 --> 00:09:57,107
(Glancing)

262
00:09:57,577 --> 00:09:59,046
(Chattering)

263
00:09:59,047 --> 00:10:00,416
- They must be his fans. - They recognized him.

264
00:10:00,417 --> 00:10:02,347
(Chattering)

265
00:10:03,187 --> 00:10:04,286
I'm sorry.

266
00:10:04,287 --> 00:10:06,716
- Are you the writer of the show? - Yes, I am.

267
00:10:06,717 --> 00:10:08,386
- Oh, my gosh. - Gosh, that's very cool.

268
00:10:08,387 --> 00:10:12,557
We're so honored to stand by you.

269
00:10:12,697 --> 00:10:14,556
I'm watching it for the second time.

270
00:10:14,557 --> 00:10:16,226
- Really? Thank you so much. - Yes. They already watched it.

271
00:10:16,227 --> 00:10:18,937
We waited in line for four hours today.

272
00:10:18,997 --> 00:10:20,067
- Oh, my gosh. - Yes.

273
00:10:20,467 --> 00:10:23,267
Yes. That's making me emotional.

274
00:10:23,907 --> 00:10:24,966
That's amazing.

275
00:10:24,967 --> 00:10:26,707
Enjoy the show. Thanks for coming.

276
00:10:27,907 --> 00:10:30,277
("Maybe Happy Ending")

277
00:10:31,547 --> 00:10:34,377
(In no time, it gets quiet in the theater.)

278
00:10:35,347 --> 00:10:37,117
(Grabbing)

279
00:10:37,987 --> 00:10:39,557
Copy, 55 seconds.

280
00:10:41,687 --> 00:10:44,527
(Fidgeting)

281
00:10:45,597 --> 00:10:49,326
Actually, I get nervous every time I watch the show.

282
00:10:49,327 --> 00:10:52,437
"What if the audience today doesn't like the show?"

283
00:10:52,737 --> 00:10:54,397
It comes with the job.

284
00:10:54,797 --> 00:10:57,706
There have been hundreds of showings so far.

285
00:10:57,707 --> 00:10:59,236
Including its performances in Korea,

286
00:10:59,237 --> 00:11:01,737
the musical has been running for ten years.

287
00:11:02,007 --> 00:11:05,877
But my palms get so sweaty when I watch the show.

288
00:11:05,977 --> 00:11:07,577
You get nervous every time?

289
00:11:07,817 --> 00:11:09,246
Strangely, yes. I can never seem...

290
00:11:09,247 --> 00:11:10,716
- to get used to it. - Really?

291
00:11:10,717 --> 00:11:12,516
No matter how many times I watch it, I'll always get nervous.

292
00:11:12,517 --> 00:11:16,026
You never know what might happen on stage.

293
00:11:16,027 --> 00:11:17,226
- Right. - It's a live performance.

294
00:11:17,227 --> 00:11:19,457
- The cast's energy differs daily. - Right.

295
00:11:21,927 --> 00:11:23,627
Gosh, I'm getting nervous for them.

296
00:11:24,597 --> 00:11:25,836
Thank you so much.

297
00:11:25,837 --> 00:11:27,397
(The last inspection before the show is complete.)

298
00:11:28,707 --> 00:11:29,807
Go.

299
00:11:31,267 --> 00:11:34,436
(The lights go out as she gives a green light.)

300
00:11:34,437 --> 00:11:35,547
Oliver.

301
00:11:37,377 --> 00:11:41,177
(The musical finally begins.)

302
00:11:43,987 --> 00:11:45,987
(The audience welcomes him with a round of applause.)

303
00:11:47,457 --> 00:11:50,387
- The vibe has a sense of freedom. - I love that!

304
00:11:51,457 --> 00:11:53,626
(Glancing)

305
00:11:53,627 --> 00:11:55,396
(Seoul, Korea)

306
00:11:55,397 --> 00:11:56,526
Oh, it's written in Korean.

307
00:11:56,527 --> 00:11:59,897
Since Korea is the setting, there are Korean subtitles too.

308
00:12:00,697 --> 00:12:02,337
Mi Do.

309
00:12:02,537 --> 00:12:05,437
(Korean characters are seen throughout the musical.)

310
00:12:06,337 --> 00:12:09,077
I'll have to model it for you later, Hwaboon.

311
00:12:09,447 --> 00:12:11,147
- Hwaboon. - Hwaboon.

312
00:12:11,707 --> 00:12:14,316
(He says the word, "flowerpot," in Korean.)

313
00:12:14,317 --> 00:12:16,446
We considered Hwaboon as one of our characters.

314
00:12:16,447 --> 00:12:18,346
So we transliterated it into Korean.

315
00:12:18,347 --> 00:12:19,816
And they loved it.

316
00:12:19,817 --> 00:12:22,327
They even gave a separate dressing room for it.

317
00:12:23,027 --> 00:12:25,426
- The flowerpot? - A dressing room for a flowerpot?

318
00:12:25,427 --> 00:12:27,596
Seeing Korean on the screen...

319
00:12:27,597 --> 00:12:29,766
- makes me feel so proud. - Right.

320
00:12:29,767 --> 00:12:31,666
- I feel a sense of patriotism. - Yes.

321
00:12:31,667 --> 00:12:33,366
Having Korean characters makes things hip these days.

322
00:12:33,367 --> 00:12:35,367
Why would you focus on that?

323
00:12:37,967 --> 00:12:40,637
(This hip musical is a story about AI robots.)

324
00:12:42,147 --> 00:12:44,107
Gosh, everyone is laughing.

325
00:12:44,407 --> 00:12:45,877
(Relieved)

326
00:12:48,317 --> 00:12:50,947
(The audience continues to laugh.)

327
00:12:51,417 --> 00:12:53,717
Hearing them laugh makes me feel good.

328
00:12:54,717 --> 00:12:57,157
(Responsive)

329
00:12:59,557 --> 00:13:00,757
The atmosphere is nice.

330
00:13:04,127 --> 00:13:06,337
(Relieved)

331
00:13:06,737 --> 00:13:09,337
(The night deepens, fueled by the audience's energy.)

332
00:13:09,537 --> 00:13:12,237
The Five Series runs out of battery really quickly.

333
00:13:13,407 --> 00:13:15,306
(In no time, they're performing the final number.)

334
00:13:15,307 --> 00:13:18,446
We at least need the Five Series. It's a fashionable design.

335
00:13:18,447 --> 00:13:19,746
The charger is losing...

336
00:13:19,747 --> 00:13:21,246
He's getting misty-eyed.

337
00:13:21,247 --> 00:13:23,516
Their acting was particularly good that day.

338
00:13:23,517 --> 00:13:24,816
I was impressed by their acting.

339
00:13:24,817 --> 00:13:27,157
So I was moved by the show even more than usual.

340
00:13:28,017 --> 00:13:29,227
I hope so.

341
00:13:33,197 --> 00:13:37,027
(The show comes to an end with a surge of heartfelt emotions.)

342
00:13:38,027 --> 00:13:40,237
(Applause)

343
00:13:45,737 --> 00:13:47,576
(Everyone in the audience gives them a standing ovation...)

344
00:13:47,577 --> 00:13:49,407
(during the curtain call.)

345
00:13:53,147 --> 00:13:54,576
(Amazed)

346
00:13:54,577 --> 00:13:55,747
There's a standing ovation.

347
00:13:57,487 --> 00:13:59,657
(They're touched too.)

348
00:14:00,287 --> 00:14:01,316
(Choking up)

349
00:14:01,317 --> 00:14:02,827
It's a tearjerker.

350
00:14:06,057 --> 00:14:08,397
(The applause goes on without end.)

351
00:14:10,627 --> 00:14:13,437
(The show ends to thunderous applause.)

352
00:14:15,337 --> 00:14:16,567
(Cheering)

353
00:14:20,277 --> 00:14:21,277
(Surprised)

354
00:14:21,278 --> 00:14:23,147
- You're tearing up. - He's tearing up.

355
00:14:24,147 --> 00:14:26,747
These feelings are difficult to describe in words.

356
00:14:27,517 --> 00:14:28,987
It feels like my heart reached them.

357
00:14:29,417 --> 00:14:34,357
In my opinion, a show requires a little bit of luck...

358
00:14:34,457 --> 00:14:37,287
and hard work of many people...

359
00:14:37,657 --> 00:14:39,826
in every role.

360
00:14:39,827 --> 00:14:42,327
And that's a rare probability.

361
00:14:42,627 --> 00:14:44,626
So it made me emotional.

362
00:14:44,627 --> 00:14:45,637
(So it made me emotional.)

363
00:14:45,967 --> 00:14:48,737
I believe the applause at the end is also meant...

364
00:14:49,067 --> 00:14:50,306
for the hard-working crew.

365
00:14:50,307 --> 00:14:52,736
When I think of all the hard work they did,

366
00:14:52,737 --> 00:14:54,777
I get even more emotional.

367
00:14:55,707 --> 00:14:59,217
(Hue leaves after the show.)

368
00:15:00,247 --> 00:15:02,347
(Lingering feelings)

369
00:15:03,447 --> 00:15:04,986
- Hey. - Hi.

370
00:15:04,987 --> 00:15:05,987
(He heads somewhere while still feeling emotional.)

371
00:15:05,988 --> 00:15:08,287
- Yes! - Hey, I cried.

372
00:15:10,587 --> 00:15:12,926
- Yes. We did it. - I want to know...

373
00:15:12,927 --> 00:15:14,397
- We did it. - We did it.

374
00:15:14,557 --> 00:15:16,667
- Gosh, that was crazy. Yes. - Great.

375
00:15:16,897 --> 00:15:18,326
Gosh, he must feel great.

376
00:15:18,327 --> 00:15:19,337
(Feeling liberated)

377
00:15:19,797 --> 00:15:21,366
How much longer will the show go on?

378
00:15:21,367 --> 00:15:23,536
- It's an open run. - Is it?

379
00:15:23,537 --> 00:15:24,936
There's no set closing date.

380
00:15:24,937 --> 00:15:26,506
The show will continue to run as long as people keep coming...

381
00:15:26,507 --> 00:15:28,076
to our show. That's the system.

382
00:15:28,077 --> 00:15:32,177
For now, tickets are available until early next year.

383
00:15:32,477 --> 00:15:33,946
There's no predetermined closing date.

384
00:15:33,947 --> 00:15:35,746
Not every show has this privilege.

385
00:15:35,747 --> 00:15:36,946
- Of course, not. - Right.

386
00:15:36,947 --> 00:15:38,587
- Some shows must close early on. - Right.

387
00:15:38,757 --> 00:15:39,887
Hi, Hue.

388
00:15:40,087 --> 00:15:41,487
Oh, my gosh.

389
00:15:43,227 --> 00:15:44,486
- Hey. - Hey, Hue.

390
00:15:44,487 --> 00:15:45,757
Where were you watching from?

391
00:15:46,427 --> 00:15:48,056
From the back, yes.

392
00:15:48,057 --> 00:15:49,667
- What? - You made me emotional.

393
00:15:50,127 --> 00:15:52,197
Yes. That's the goal.

394
00:15:53,397 --> 00:15:55,337
I liked what you did.

395
00:15:55,767 --> 00:15:57,007
"Okay, goodbye." And then...

396
00:15:57,067 --> 00:16:01,277
You did something like that today. And that was funny.

397
00:16:02,977 --> 00:16:05,617
- Right, exactly. Yes. - Yes. That was a joke.

398
00:16:06,017 --> 00:16:08,647
- Well, you were fantastic. - The atmosphere looks good.

399
00:16:08,887 --> 00:16:11,146
She improvised and did a new sound effect.

400
00:16:11,147 --> 00:16:13,457
She wanted to know if it worked or not.

401
00:16:13,617 --> 00:16:15,957
So I told her it was funny.

402
00:16:17,087 --> 00:16:18,827
Don't party too hard without us.

403
00:16:19,197 --> 00:16:20,497
- That's the male lead. - It's just in the house.

404
00:16:20,797 --> 00:16:23,366
It's the perfect day to look out the window.

405
00:16:23,367 --> 00:16:24,567
(He won the Best Performance by a Leading Actor.)

406
00:16:25,397 --> 00:16:27,896
Yes. I knew he looked familiar.

407
00:16:27,897 --> 00:16:30,067
- It was a great show. Really. - Yes. Thanks a lot.

408
00:16:30,167 --> 00:16:32,206
I mean, this literally happened.

409
00:16:32,207 --> 00:16:34,236
This guy from the audience who was standing next to me said,

410
00:16:34,237 --> 00:16:35,946
"This is my second time."

411
00:16:35,947 --> 00:16:37,506
They came here at 6am...

412
00:16:37,507 --> 00:16:39,117
- Just to catch the show? - to watch...

413
00:16:39,317 --> 00:16:41,246
- To watch the show again. - Hopefully, it was a good show.

414
00:16:41,247 --> 00:16:42,547
I hope we did okay.

415
00:16:42,717 --> 00:16:45,347
I mean, even that made me super emotional.

416
00:16:45,487 --> 00:16:47,286
- How she was so engaged... - Yes.

417
00:16:47,287 --> 00:16:48,487
With the work...

418
00:16:48,887 --> 00:16:49,956
Let me give you a big hug.

419
00:16:49,957 --> 00:16:50,957
- I just love and adore you. - All right.

420
00:16:50,958 --> 00:16:52,726
I'm so grateful for you. I'll miss you, man.

421
00:16:52,727 --> 00:16:54,326
- Yes. - Did you like the show?

422
00:16:54,327 --> 00:16:56,727
- Yes. It was great. - It was a good show.

423
00:16:56,867 --> 00:16:58,266
It was a pretty good show.

424
00:16:58,267 --> 00:16:59,666
- We did good on it. - I'm joking. Yes.

425
00:16:59,667 --> 00:17:00,966
It was a great show.

426
00:17:00,967 --> 00:17:03,136
- It made me emotional. Good job. - Yes.

427
00:17:03,137 --> 00:17:04,607
"Emotional." He uses that word a lot.

428
00:17:04,837 --> 00:17:07,136
- I use it to say I'm touched... - You're moved.

429
00:17:07,137 --> 00:17:09,976
And moved. That's when I use that word.

430
00:17:09,977 --> 00:17:11,676
But it might have to do...

431
00:17:11,677 --> 00:17:13,417
- with the plot of our show. - What?

432
00:17:13,477 --> 00:17:16,817
A lot of the cast members and the audience use that word.

433
00:17:17,287 --> 00:17:20,987
Here's the thing. Certain shows deplete your energy afterward.

434
00:17:21,287 --> 00:17:23,086
But some shows actually give you more energy...

435
00:17:23,087 --> 00:17:25,427
- even after performing for hours. - Right.

436
00:17:25,497 --> 00:17:29,327
"Maybe Happy Ending" must give more energy to people afterward.

437
00:17:31,327 --> 00:17:32,767
(The emotional musical comes to an end.)

438
00:17:33,767 --> 00:17:36,736
(A crowd has formed in front of the theater.)

439
00:17:36,737 --> 00:17:37,866
- Why are there so many people? - What?

440
00:17:37,867 --> 00:17:40,107
- They're waiting for the cast. - To see them get off work?

441
00:17:40,537 --> 00:17:41,676
- My gosh. - I didn't know...

442
00:17:41,677 --> 00:17:42,807
this culture existed there too.

443
00:17:42,977 --> 00:17:44,307
My gosh.

444
00:17:45,177 --> 00:17:46,217
They're so kind.

445
00:17:47,047 --> 00:17:49,517
(Grabbing)

446
00:17:51,417 --> 00:17:53,516
(Hue quietly gets off work by himself.)

447
00:17:53,517 --> 00:17:55,987
I usually run away from the crowd quietly.

448
00:17:56,887 --> 00:17:59,626
When they stop me there, I feel so awkward.

449
00:17:59,627 --> 00:18:02,927
I usually run out of there fast with my hat on.

450
00:18:03,297 --> 00:18:05,067
That's how an artist ought to be.

451
00:18:05,497 --> 00:18:06,537
(Chuckling)

452
00:18:06,837 --> 00:18:07,966
(Smirking)

453
00:18:07,967 --> 00:18:10,437
- What do you mean? - But I became an attention-seeker.

454
00:18:10,807 --> 00:18:12,677
"How come there's no one here?" Like this?

455
00:18:12,777 --> 00:18:14,406
- Yes. - He held a solo exhibit...

456
00:18:14,407 --> 00:18:15,646
in Seongsu-dong.

457
00:18:15,647 --> 00:18:17,147
He was so self-conscious around people.

458
00:18:17,807 --> 00:18:19,246
He showed up in front of them...

459
00:18:19,247 --> 00:18:21,047
on his way to work, after work, and during his breaks.

460
00:18:21,147 --> 00:18:22,886
You stood there for a long time too.

461
00:18:22,887 --> 00:18:24,547
"How come they can't recognize me?"

462
00:18:25,817 --> 00:18:28,156
- He's so self-aware. Yes. - Right, he is.

463
00:18:28,157 --> 00:18:29,427
(He's so self-aware.)

464
00:18:30,127 --> 00:18:31,686
(Hue quickly gets off work.)

465
00:18:31,687 --> 00:18:33,226
- That's cool. - My gosh.

466
00:18:33,227 --> 00:18:35,497
He's trudging back home after a successful show.

467
00:18:41,137 --> 00:18:42,166
(He enters a nearby subway station.)

468
00:18:42,167 --> 00:18:43,807
He's taking the subway home.

469
00:18:44,737 --> 00:18:47,977
(He hurriedly passes through the turnstile.)

470
00:18:49,477 --> 00:18:52,517
(A New York subway train enters the platform in no time.)

471
00:18:53,677 --> 00:18:57,187
(I must hurry back home.)

472
00:18:58,347 --> 00:19:00,757
(Opening)

473
00:19:02,087 --> 00:19:04,957
(He opens up a book as soon as he sits down.)

474
00:19:05,697 --> 00:19:09,197
(Hue is instantly engrossed in the book.)

475
00:19:11,527 --> 00:19:13,366
- How long does it take to get home? - About 15 minutes.

476
00:19:13,367 --> 00:19:14,697
That's pretty close.

477
00:19:14,737 --> 00:19:16,767
The rent must be quite expensive.

478
00:19:16,867 --> 00:19:19,537
The rent in my neighborhood is quite reasonable.

479
00:19:20,077 --> 00:19:23,147
The next stop is 52nd Street.

480
00:19:24,677 --> 00:19:27,717
(Getting up)

481
00:19:30,687 --> 00:19:33,816
(Rushing)

482
00:19:33,817 --> 00:19:35,017
It's quiet.

483
00:19:35,757 --> 00:19:37,226
(Unlike Broadway, his neighborhood is quiet.)

484
00:19:37,227 --> 00:19:38,526
The vibe changed completely.

485
00:19:38,527 --> 00:19:40,727
- It reminds me of Seongsu-dong. - Yes.

486
00:19:41,497 --> 00:19:43,126
It's a quiet residential area.

487
00:19:43,127 --> 00:19:44,727
So there are rarely people out on the street at night.

488
00:19:46,137 --> 00:19:47,236
(He lives in Queens, 15 minutes away from Broadway.)

489
00:19:47,237 --> 00:19:48,737
I see. He lives in Queens.

490
00:19:48,797 --> 00:19:51,107
That's where Spider-Man lives.

491
00:19:52,167 --> 00:19:56,477
(When he walks down an alley on the outskirts of New York City, )

492
00:19:57,277 --> 00:20:00,447
(he arrives at his sweet home right away.)

493
00:20:00,877 --> 00:20:02,747
(Creaking)

494
00:20:07,287 --> 00:20:09,756
This building is 100 years old.

495
00:20:09,757 --> 00:20:12,826
I live on the fourth floor which is the top floor.

496
00:20:12,827 --> 00:20:15,327
- Without the elevator? - There's no elevator.

497
00:20:16,397 --> 00:20:17,826
(The building has no elevator, )

498
00:20:17,827 --> 00:20:18,896
(and he must use a key to open it like the old-fashioned way.)

499
00:20:18,897 --> 00:20:20,636
That's how it is in the US.

500
00:20:20,637 --> 00:20:22,067
They still use those old-school keys.

501
00:20:23,137 --> 00:20:24,207
Alexa.

502
00:20:25,207 --> 00:20:26,676
Turn on all the Hue lights.

503
00:20:26,677 --> 00:20:28,337
- What? - Do you have a roommate?

504
00:20:29,047 --> 00:20:30,606
Alexa is AI.

505
00:20:30,607 --> 00:20:31,707
AI?

506
00:20:31,877 --> 00:20:33,646
He's using AI right after we said he used an old-school key.

507
00:20:33,647 --> 00:20:35,217
(There's a smart home within an old building.)

508
00:20:37,187 --> 00:20:39,116
(Flinging open)

509
00:20:39,117 --> 00:20:40,857
(He stays busy as soon as he arrives.)

510
00:20:41,717 --> 00:20:43,787
(Gushing)

511
00:20:44,387 --> 00:20:46,056
(He fills up the pot with water and puts it on the stove.)

512
00:20:46,057 --> 00:20:47,256
That's when you get hungry.

513
00:20:47,257 --> 00:20:49,197
I do that as soon as I come home.

514
00:20:50,097 --> 00:20:52,327
(Let me see.)

515
00:20:53,197 --> 00:20:54,197
- What? - Gosh.

516
00:20:54,198 --> 00:20:56,167
- The rustling sound means ramyeon. - That's a bag of ramyeon.

517
00:20:57,867 --> 00:20:59,336
- It's Neoguri. - Neoguri.

518
00:20:59,337 --> 00:21:00,476
(Neoguri Ramyeon)

519
00:21:00,477 --> 00:21:02,407
That's the US version of it.

520
00:21:04,047 --> 00:21:05,276
(His favorite after-work snack is ramyeon.)

521
00:21:05,277 --> 00:21:07,076
- You sometimes crave it. - Right.

522
00:21:07,077 --> 00:21:08,146
- You crave it. - Yes.

523
00:21:08,147 --> 00:21:10,716
Here's what musical actors and actresses do.

524
00:21:10,717 --> 00:21:11,946
Let's say Act One just ended.

525
00:21:11,947 --> 00:21:13,317
This is what we talk about during the intermission.

526
00:21:13,487 --> 00:21:14,616
"Hey, what will you get today?"

527
00:21:14,617 --> 00:21:15,956
- "What kind of food?" - Right.

528
00:21:15,957 --> 00:21:17,956
- We talk about food a lot. - I see. After the show?

529
00:21:17,957 --> 00:21:20,097
Hyun Moo actually eats food instead of talking about it.

530
00:21:21,197 --> 00:21:22,197
Why think about it?

531
00:21:22,198 --> 00:21:24,297
Use that time to have one more bite.

532
00:21:26,297 --> 00:21:27,337
(Rustling)

533
00:21:27,997 --> 00:21:28,997
What's that?

534
00:21:28,998 --> 00:21:30,037
(Seaweed?)

535
00:21:33,177 --> 00:21:36,207
(He adds seaweed to the water, meant for the ramyeon.)

536
00:21:36,347 --> 00:21:37,646
It doesn't come with the kelp.

537
00:21:37,647 --> 00:21:39,317
So I started adding seaweed to it.

538
00:21:39,677 --> 00:21:40,917
- Oh, my gosh. - Why?

539
00:21:41,017 --> 00:21:42,586
Then it's not real Neoguri.

540
00:21:42,587 --> 00:21:43,647
- That's not right. - Right.

541
00:21:44,247 --> 00:21:46,956
(The US version has different ingredients from the original one.)

542
00:21:46,957 --> 00:21:48,586
The US version doesn't have kelp.

543
00:21:48,587 --> 00:21:49,956
(This news had gone viral a while ago.)

544
00:21:49,957 --> 00:21:50,957
My gosh, this is...

545
00:21:51,557 --> 00:21:53,326
But that must be such a disappointment.

546
00:21:53,327 --> 00:21:54,827
I was so disappointed at first.

547
00:21:55,197 --> 00:21:58,126
I was very flustered when I first came here 18 years ago.

548
00:21:58,127 --> 00:21:59,967
I thought it was a defective product initially.

549
00:22:00,297 --> 00:22:02,767
So I bought another one, but it was the same.

550
00:22:03,167 --> 00:22:05,707
That's why I add seaweed to it.

551
00:22:06,507 --> 00:22:07,737
I thought it was someone's birthday.

552
00:22:07,777 --> 00:22:08,836
He added a lot too.

553
00:22:08,837 --> 00:22:10,677
That's enough for a mom who just gave birth.

554
00:22:14,077 --> 00:22:15,787
(He adds every particle of the spicy seasoning.)

555
00:22:16,947 --> 00:22:19,357
(He recreates the flavor from home.)

556
00:22:20,217 --> 00:22:21,287
What about kimchi?

557
00:22:22,387 --> 00:22:23,387
He has it.

558
00:22:23,387 --> 00:22:24,387
(Kimchi completes the dish.)

559
00:22:24,388 --> 00:22:26,427
- He's Korean, you know? - This proves he's Korean indeed.

560
00:22:26,527 --> 00:22:28,097
- Right. - He needs kimchi, of course.

561
00:22:28,527 --> 00:22:30,397
(It's ready.)

562
00:22:31,467 --> 00:22:33,967
(He quickly sets the table, so the noodles don't get soggy.)

563
00:22:35,737 --> 00:22:37,036
(Even the color looks authentic.)

564
00:22:37,037 --> 00:22:38,307
It looks tasty.

565
00:22:38,437 --> 00:22:39,836
Neoguri with seaweed looks pretty good.

566
00:22:39,837 --> 00:22:42,307
(A meal of a Korean guy in New York)

567
00:22:42,677 --> 00:22:44,347
(Let's eat now.)

568
00:22:45,047 --> 00:22:46,977
(Stirring)

569
00:22:49,787 --> 00:22:50,817
(Cooling)

570
00:22:51,247 --> 00:22:52,247
(Slurping)

571
00:22:52,617 --> 00:22:53,687
That looks good.

572
00:22:53,757 --> 00:22:54,887
(Slurping)

573
00:22:56,057 --> 00:22:57,086
(Yes, this is it!)

574
00:22:57,087 --> 00:22:58,326
That hits the spot.

575
00:22:58,327 --> 00:23:00,597
Having ramyeon after the show hits the spot.

576
00:23:02,057 --> 00:23:05,627
(The taste of Korea fills his hungry stomach after the show.)

577
00:23:07,497 --> 00:23:09,467
(Captivated)

578
00:23:09,807 --> 00:23:12,137
(This is so good.)

579
00:23:12,407 --> 00:23:13,436
(Staring)

580
00:23:13,437 --> 00:23:15,277
You need a funny video to watch while eating.

581
00:23:16,447 --> 00:23:18,047
(Chuckling)

582
00:23:18,947 --> 00:23:20,046
- Isn't that us? - That's Na Rae's laugh.

583
00:23:20,047 --> 00:23:21,077
What?

584
00:23:21,277 --> 00:23:23,187
Mr. Ahn wrote a comment right away.

585
00:23:23,347 --> 00:23:24,916
- My goodness. - "Can you travel..."

586
00:23:24,917 --> 00:23:26,186
- "for work?" - That's me!

587
00:23:26,187 --> 00:23:27,316
People watch me abroad!

588
00:23:27,317 --> 00:23:29,087
- The broom-to-be. - The broom-to-be.

589
00:23:30,957 --> 00:23:32,597
(He's getting into it.)

590
00:23:33,997 --> 00:23:37,167
(Na Rae is enjoying her life.)

591
00:23:38,467 --> 00:23:39,567
That's the camouflage cream.

592
00:23:39,667 --> 00:23:40,997
(Chuckling)

593
00:23:41,367 --> 00:23:42,567
That's the camouflage cream.

594
00:23:43,137 --> 00:23:46,436
(Giggling)

595
00:23:46,437 --> 00:23:48,836
After the show, I come home at night...

596
00:23:48,837 --> 00:23:50,576
and eat ramyeon.

597
00:23:50,577 --> 00:23:52,407
I watch Korean variety shows too.

598
00:23:52,647 --> 00:23:53,677
At least in that moment,

599
00:23:54,147 --> 00:23:57,016
doing that alleviates my stress.

600
00:23:57,017 --> 00:23:58,087
It's fleeting, though.

601
00:23:58,787 --> 00:24:00,487
(Nothing is stressing him out while watching Na Rae's day.)

602
00:24:01,287 --> 00:24:03,457
I'm not sure. I have to see it through to know.

603
00:24:03,757 --> 00:24:05,157
My gosh, he finished everything.

604
00:24:06,757 --> 00:24:08,427
(He keeps watching it in the living room and enjoys it.)

605
00:24:09,027 --> 00:24:11,466
(Laughing)

606
00:24:11,467 --> 00:24:13,137
I really enjoyed that episode.

607
00:24:13,267 --> 00:24:15,437
- That's... The broom-to-be. - The broom-to-be is in New York.

608
00:24:16,807 --> 00:24:18,506
(The seaweed-wrapped broom-to-be has followed him to New York.)

609
00:24:18,507 --> 00:24:19,806
We have to watch that again?

610
00:24:19,807 --> 00:24:22,077
I kept watching that while having seaweed.

611
00:24:22,747 --> 00:24:24,447
Dive into Neoguri.

612
00:24:25,247 --> 00:24:27,317
I can't tell from where her dress starts.

613
00:24:28,077 --> 00:24:29,546
(As he laughs with the Rainbow Club members...)

614
00:24:29,547 --> 00:24:31,446
That episode was really funny.

615
00:24:31,447 --> 00:24:33,686
Your broom-to-be episode made him happy.

616
00:24:33,687 --> 00:24:34,787
(Touched)

617
00:24:36,257 --> 00:24:40,597
(His hectic day in New York has ended.)

618
00:24:41,727 --> 00:24:42,727
(Next day)

619
00:24:42,728 --> 00:24:44,897
I'm curious about his morning.

620
00:24:45,937 --> 00:24:49,837
(Hue is still fast asleep.)

621
00:24:55,337 --> 00:24:56,376
(An old-fashioned alarm clock)

622
00:24:56,377 --> 00:24:57,647
That's how an American alarm clock sounds.

623
00:24:58,077 --> 00:24:59,347
What do you mean? An American alarm clock?

624
00:25:00,947 --> 00:25:03,987
(He doesn't even budge.)

625
00:25:05,387 --> 00:25:07,117
(Beeping rapidly)

626
00:25:08,517 --> 00:25:10,357
(The American alarm clock is going off ruthlessly.)

627
00:25:10,827 --> 00:25:13,327
Seriously. It's a traditional alarm sound.

628
00:25:14,057 --> 00:25:15,726
(Beeping rapidly)

629
00:25:15,727 --> 00:25:16,967
Gosh, that's no joke.

630
00:25:17,227 --> 00:25:18,997
(The old-fashioned alarm sound wakes him up.)

631
00:25:22,767 --> 00:25:25,766
I left my smartphone in the living room on purpose.

632
00:25:25,767 --> 00:25:29,006
I started to put only the alarm clock in my bedroom.

633
00:25:29,007 --> 00:25:30,806
- Gosh, that's not easy. - Then...

634
00:25:30,807 --> 00:25:32,846
you don't check your phone before going to sleep?

635
00:25:32,847 --> 00:25:34,046
I'm trying not to do that.

636
00:25:34,047 --> 00:25:35,746
Having my phone in the bedroom distracts me...

637
00:25:35,747 --> 00:25:36,946
- from sleep. - That's right.

638
00:25:36,947 --> 00:25:37,986
When I'm on my phone,

639
00:25:37,987 --> 00:25:39,217
- I struggle to fall asleep. - Right.

640
00:25:40,157 --> 00:25:42,117
(He wakes up at the sound of his clock, not his phone.)

641
00:25:43,357 --> 00:25:44,757
(Grabbing)

642
00:25:47,657 --> 00:25:48,897
A robe.

643
00:25:49,697 --> 00:25:50,797
The American vibe.

644
00:25:51,127 --> 00:25:52,737
"The American vibe?"

645
00:25:53,437 --> 00:25:54,867
(Drawing)

646
00:25:55,267 --> 00:25:57,007
Check out how well-lit his room is.

647
00:25:57,267 --> 00:25:59,606
Since I live on the top floor, my room gets a lot of sunlight.

648
00:25:59,607 --> 00:26:02,776
Since I spend a long time working from home,

649
00:26:02,777 --> 00:26:05,607
I'm sensitive to noise between floors.

650
00:26:06,847 --> 00:26:08,777
(Fumbling)

651
00:26:11,947 --> 00:26:13,787
- Gosh, your face looks so puffy. - Oh, no.

652
00:26:13,887 --> 00:26:15,356
You now have the face of a raccoon.

653
00:26:15,357 --> 00:26:16,827
Gosh, I can't believe it. No way.

654
00:26:17,387 --> 00:26:19,187
They started to pick on him now.

655
00:26:19,257 --> 00:26:21,697
They're teasing him now that he's not at the theater.

656
00:26:21,797 --> 00:26:22,797
(This is so fun.)

657
00:26:23,127 --> 00:26:24,327
(Utterly nervous)

658
00:26:24,967 --> 00:26:27,267
(He goes somewhere after turning into a raccoon.)

659
00:26:28,767 --> 00:26:30,807
(I should wash up briefly.)

660
00:26:33,037 --> 00:26:34,577
(Refreshed)

661
00:26:35,537 --> 00:26:37,947
(He comes back to the bedroom after washing up.)

662
00:26:40,477 --> 00:26:42,217
(Grabbing)

663
00:26:43,287 --> 00:26:44,347
(Pounding)

664
00:26:44,547 --> 00:26:46,787
(He immediately makes the bed.)

665
00:26:48,417 --> 00:26:49,986
(Pulling hard)

666
00:26:49,987 --> 00:26:51,056
(Tight)

667
00:26:51,057 --> 00:26:53,327
- That's how it's done at hotels. - At hotels.

668
00:26:53,697 --> 00:26:54,996
It's an old habit of mine.

669
00:26:54,997 --> 00:26:57,066
Since I work from home,

670
00:26:57,067 --> 00:26:59,327
- You're a neat guy. - I want my house to be tidy.

671
00:27:00,037 --> 00:27:01,066
(Looking neat)

672
00:27:01,067 --> 00:27:02,067
(I'm done.)

673
00:27:05,867 --> 00:27:07,337
It's nice.

674
00:27:08,107 --> 00:27:12,207
The house I live in is located in Queens, New York.

675
00:27:12,677 --> 00:27:14,146
I lived in Brooklyn for many years,

676
00:27:14,147 --> 00:27:16,517
but moved here during COVID-19.

677
00:27:18,247 --> 00:27:20,086
(Welcome to Hue's house.)

678
00:27:20,087 --> 00:27:21,517
It's bigger than I expected.

679
00:27:23,557 --> 00:27:24,887
It's perfect for one person.

680
00:27:25,457 --> 00:27:27,696
In the US, it's called a one-bedroom apartment.

681
00:27:27,697 --> 00:27:30,826
It's a typical apartment with one bedroom.

682
00:27:30,827 --> 00:27:32,667
The living room is at the end.

683
00:27:32,867 --> 00:27:34,197
Tony Awards.

684
00:27:35,497 --> 00:27:37,607
- It's so neat. - It's cozy.

685
00:27:38,967 --> 00:27:42,736
There are a kitchen, a restroom, and a living room.

686
00:27:42,737 --> 00:27:45,877
I think it's a very fitting place for me.

687
00:27:46,077 --> 00:27:47,646
The Brooklyn apartment I lived in for many years...

688
00:27:47,647 --> 00:27:49,587
was a studio apartment.

689
00:27:49,887 --> 00:27:52,086
When I moved here, I got a living room.

690
00:27:52,087 --> 00:27:53,356
I got a bedroom with a door.

691
00:27:53,357 --> 00:27:55,056
When I slept with the door closed,

692
00:27:55,057 --> 00:27:57,086
I felt very successful.

693
00:27:57,087 --> 00:27:59,857
"Now, I can sleep with the door closed."

694
00:27:59,997 --> 00:28:02,557
Those who live in a studio apartment will agree.

695
00:28:02,757 --> 00:28:03,927
It's nice when the space is divided.

696
00:28:04,097 --> 00:28:05,726
Since you've won the Tony Awards,

697
00:28:05,727 --> 00:28:07,236
you should begin looking into properties.

698
00:28:07,237 --> 00:28:09,706
But Tony Awards don't come with a money prize.

699
00:28:09,707 --> 00:28:12,406
I also haven't been paid for Broadway performances yet.

700
00:28:12,407 --> 00:28:13,737
- What? - Why not?

701
00:28:13,837 --> 00:28:15,477
- Hold on. - Payments take a long time.

702
00:28:16,147 --> 00:28:17,706
When will you receive the money?

703
00:28:17,707 --> 00:28:19,207
It's done every quarter of a year.

704
00:28:19,347 --> 00:28:21,877
In the first quarter, the musical didn't make any profits.

705
00:28:22,377 --> 00:28:24,417
Thus, I didn't get paid.

706
00:28:24,647 --> 00:28:26,416
The payments for the 2nd and 3rd quarters...

707
00:28:26,417 --> 00:28:29,357
will be made soon.

708
00:28:29,927 --> 00:28:32,257
Do you go and count the audience members every day?

709
00:28:32,627 --> 00:28:33,756
- Do you calculate the profits? - That's right.

710
00:28:33,757 --> 00:28:34,796
- I bet he takes photos. - Yes.

711
00:28:34,797 --> 00:28:36,667
There's no chance of them not paying you, right?

712
00:28:36,897 --> 00:28:38,426
- It won't happen. - I believe they'll pay me.

713
00:28:38,427 --> 00:28:40,196
- We signed a contract. - That's right.

714
00:28:40,197 --> 00:28:41,936
- All right. - They give me the minimum amount.

715
00:28:41,937 --> 00:28:44,367
Every week, they give me the minimum amount.

716
00:28:44,537 --> 00:28:46,176
I expected better from them,

717
00:28:46,177 --> 00:28:48,307
but New Yorkers didn't uphold their promises.

718
00:28:48,737 --> 00:28:50,546
You must be thinking about the New York-based newspaper.

719
00:28:50,547 --> 00:28:51,677
The New York-based newspaper.

720
00:28:52,347 --> 00:28:53,916
- Because of the newspaper? - He's very angry about it.

721
00:28:53,917 --> 00:28:55,646
If they don't give him his money, let's send Sung Hwan.

722
00:28:55,647 --> 00:28:56,917
I'll go there as a witness.

723
00:28:58,017 --> 00:28:59,757
You just want an excuse to go to New York.

724
00:29:00,387 --> 00:29:03,327
I spend a lot of time at home.

725
00:29:03,557 --> 00:29:06,496
I can't focus if there's too much going on.

726
00:29:06,497 --> 00:29:10,566
I stick to the white and wood tones.

727
00:29:10,567 --> 00:29:12,467
It's like a house you see in a magazine.

728
00:29:12,867 --> 00:29:15,137
There's another plant. Hwaboon.

729
00:29:15,337 --> 00:29:17,267
- Hwaboon. - I'll explain later, Hwaboon.

730
00:29:18,307 --> 00:29:20,277
I have many plants at home.

731
00:29:20,537 --> 00:29:22,877
The house is similar to yesterday's stage.

732
00:29:24,377 --> 00:29:27,416
- You must be sensitive to colors. - That's right.

733
00:29:27,417 --> 00:29:29,046
I graduated from an art university.

734
00:29:29,047 --> 00:29:30,316
- You studied the arts. - Yes.

735
00:29:30,317 --> 00:29:32,847
In Korea, I majored in creative writing.

736
00:29:32,957 --> 00:29:35,517
I went to the US to study the arts.

737
00:29:35,617 --> 00:29:36,826
After majoring in the arts,

738
00:29:36,827 --> 00:29:39,227
I worked at a marketing company as a designer.

739
00:29:39,657 --> 00:29:42,696
Since I was used to writing,

740
00:29:42,697 --> 00:29:46,196
I decided to continue writing my own works.

741
00:29:46,197 --> 00:29:48,197
That was why I began writing at home after work.

742
00:29:48,767 --> 00:29:51,407
My works got turned into musicals in Korea.

743
00:29:51,567 --> 00:29:53,636
Then I became a full-time writer.

744
00:29:53,637 --> 00:29:54,707
How fascinating.

745
00:29:55,007 --> 00:29:56,477
- He's a talented writer. - Yes.

746
00:29:57,707 --> 00:29:59,717
(Hue comes back to the kitchen.)

747
00:30:00,647 --> 00:30:01,916
(He sits at the table.)

748
00:30:01,917 --> 00:30:03,587
(Grabbing)

749
00:30:04,417 --> 00:30:07,187
(He enjoys a cup of fragrant coffee and a book.)

750
00:30:09,287 --> 00:30:10,786
- He reads a lot. - Gosh.

751
00:30:10,787 --> 00:30:11,826
(Gosh, he's cool!)

752
00:30:11,827 --> 00:30:13,027
It shows that he's a writer.

753
00:30:13,397 --> 00:30:14,896
That's the New Yorker's life we dream of.

754
00:30:14,897 --> 00:30:16,096
Had we been in New York, we would've read books.

755
00:30:16,097 --> 00:30:18,026
Of course. I would've read books.

756
00:30:18,027 --> 00:30:19,697
- The sunlight is different. - I would've read every day.

757
00:30:21,937 --> 00:30:23,407
It's a routine of mine.

758
00:30:24,107 --> 00:30:27,207
In the morning, I read a book in Korean.

759
00:30:27,737 --> 00:30:29,506
In the afternoon or at night,

760
00:30:29,507 --> 00:30:32,047
I read a book in English. It became a habit.

761
00:30:32,777 --> 00:30:33,847
How cool.

762
00:30:34,077 --> 00:30:35,346
Ever since I was little,

763
00:30:35,347 --> 00:30:37,316
- I've had a reading addiction. - A reading addiction.

764
00:30:37,317 --> 00:30:39,717
Even in elementary school,

765
00:30:39,917 --> 00:30:42,086
I couldn't go to the restroom without a book.

766
00:30:42,087 --> 00:30:44,227
It was like a condition.

767
00:30:44,427 --> 00:30:47,056
As I get older, I think that this ability is like a muscle.

768
00:30:47,057 --> 00:30:48,457
If you stop reading,

769
00:30:48,857 --> 00:30:50,466
- you keep reading the same part. - That's right.

770
00:30:50,467 --> 00:30:52,567
"What did they say earlier?" I have to go back and check.

771
00:30:52,867 --> 00:30:55,066
You need to keep reading...

772
00:30:55,067 --> 00:30:56,936
to work the muscles in your brain.

773
00:30:56,937 --> 00:30:58,837
I make an effort to be careful.

774
00:30:59,977 --> 00:31:01,406
(Hue's morning routine that protects his brain health...)

775
00:31:01,407 --> 00:31:02,807
(is reading.)

776
00:31:02,937 --> 00:31:05,006
When I look at the smartphone,

777
00:31:05,007 --> 00:31:06,046
I feel like...

778
00:31:06,047 --> 00:31:07,916
- my brain is slowing down. - That's right.

779
00:31:07,917 --> 00:31:09,447
Only the cellphones are becoming smarter.

780
00:31:09,547 --> 00:31:10,617
Yes.

781
00:31:11,847 --> 00:31:13,586
Reading in the morning is great.

782
00:31:13,587 --> 00:31:15,757
(Hue finishes reading to make himself smarter.)

783
00:31:17,157 --> 00:31:18,686
(He rinses the cup he has used right away.)

784
00:31:18,687 --> 00:31:20,357
He cleans up right away.

785
00:31:20,827 --> 00:31:22,757
Do you do the dirty dishes right away?

786
00:31:22,957 --> 00:31:25,366
Yes. There are too many cockroaches in the US.

787
00:31:25,367 --> 00:31:26,367
- I see. - New York is like that.

788
00:31:26,368 --> 00:31:27,736
If I don't clean up,

789
00:31:27,737 --> 00:31:29,237
I get insects in the apartment.

790
00:31:30,067 --> 00:31:33,707
(He does the dirty dishes so that there won't be any insects.)

791
00:31:35,737 --> 00:31:37,547
(Opening)

792
00:31:38,577 --> 00:31:41,647
(Hue takes something out.)

793
00:31:43,247 --> 00:31:44,287
A mat?

794
00:31:44,517 --> 00:31:46,047
- Will he work out at home? - Is it a yoga mat?

795
00:31:46,557 --> 00:31:47,817
Will he work out now?

796
00:31:49,657 --> 00:31:52,927
(He takes off the robe he has been wearing.)

797
00:31:55,297 --> 00:31:56,396
(Plank)

798
00:31:56,397 --> 00:31:57,567
- Plank. - Plank.

799
00:31:58,027 --> 00:31:59,267
For how many minutes will he do it?

800
00:31:59,797 --> 00:32:01,167
(Smiling)

801
00:32:02,397 --> 00:32:06,376
(Heave-ho)

802
00:32:06,377 --> 00:32:09,436
It's embarrassing to call it an at-home workout.

803
00:32:09,437 --> 00:32:12,006
These days, my posture is so bad...

804
00:32:12,007 --> 00:32:13,416
- that my back... - Right. It's for his posture.

805
00:32:13,417 --> 00:32:14,747
And neck hurt.

806
00:32:14,917 --> 00:32:17,186
So it's my goal to exercise even if it's for 15 to 20 minutes.

807
00:32:17,187 --> 00:32:18,946
I don't have much time,

808
00:32:18,947 --> 00:32:21,416
so I do it briefly in the morning.

809
00:32:21,417 --> 00:32:22,817
(A simple exercise after reading is a part of the morning routine.)

810
00:32:23,757 --> 00:32:25,627
(Clenching his jaw)

811
00:32:27,597 --> 00:32:29,327
His legs aren't half bad.

812
00:32:30,027 --> 00:32:31,666
(He moves on to the upper body exercise.)

813
00:32:31,667 --> 00:32:33,136
Back in the day,

814
00:32:33,137 --> 00:32:34,736
I did weightlifting.

815
00:32:34,737 --> 00:32:35,967
- What? - Yes.

816
00:32:36,837 --> 00:32:39,807
(Did Writer Hue Park do weightlifting?)

817
00:32:40,077 --> 00:32:42,406
Back in the day, I did weightlifting.

818
00:32:42,407 --> 00:32:43,577
- What? - Yes.

819
00:32:43,907 --> 00:32:45,776
- I joined a team. - Weightlifting?

820
00:32:45,777 --> 00:32:47,346
He really enjoyed his life.

821
00:32:47,347 --> 00:32:48,347
I wasn't a professional.

822
00:32:48,348 --> 00:32:50,516
I joined a weightlifting team in the neighborhood.

823
00:32:50,517 --> 00:32:51,987
- Was it like a club? - Yes.

824
00:32:52,517 --> 00:32:53,556
(A few years ago, when he was in a weightlifting crew)

825
00:32:53,557 --> 00:32:55,686
- All right. - That's a bit...

826
00:32:55,687 --> 00:32:58,287
- Is that Hue? - Yes. He's good.

827
00:32:58,457 --> 00:33:00,297
- This is unexpected. - Yes.

828
00:33:00,557 --> 00:33:01,797
He has so many different sides.

829
00:33:02,357 --> 00:33:03,996
(He brings out his skills from the past.)

830
00:33:03,997 --> 00:33:05,127
- Nice. - That's...

831
00:33:05,897 --> 00:33:08,567
(Morning strength exercise)

832
00:33:10,337 --> 00:33:12,437
I thought he'd like aerobic exercise.

833
00:33:12,937 --> 00:33:14,206
I don't like aerobic exercise.

834
00:33:14,207 --> 00:33:15,437
You like weightlifting.

835
00:33:16,607 --> 00:33:18,507
(My goodness)

836
00:33:19,507 --> 00:33:20,517
(Panting)

837
00:33:21,417 --> 00:33:22,417
(Panting)

838
00:33:23,117 --> 00:33:24,717
I became tired after ten minutes.

839
00:33:24,847 --> 00:33:26,317
- Did you exercise for ten minutes? - Yes.

840
00:33:27,357 --> 00:33:28,357
I...

841
00:33:29,187 --> 00:33:31,557
(He's exhausted after exercising for 10 minutes.)

842
00:33:32,227 --> 00:33:34,157
(Covered in sweat)

843
00:33:36,157 --> 00:33:39,067
(He rubs a facial cleanser on his face.)

844
00:33:40,897 --> 00:33:43,436
(Then he prepares a washcloth right away.)

845
00:33:43,437 --> 00:33:44,466
What are you doing?

846
00:33:44,467 --> 00:33:46,437
In the US, the bathroom floor is kept dry.

847
00:33:47,237 --> 00:33:48,637
That's why you don't splash water.

848
00:33:48,777 --> 00:33:50,006
That's why I use a washcloth.

849
00:33:50,007 --> 00:33:51,146
- Really? - Yes.

850
00:33:51,147 --> 00:33:53,547
I wet a washcloth in water...

851
00:33:54,177 --> 00:33:55,517
and wipe my face.

852
00:33:56,447 --> 00:33:58,247
Then it keeps the water from splashing on the floor.

853
00:33:58,447 --> 00:34:00,017
That makes sense.

854
00:34:01,217 --> 00:34:04,187
I learned it from my American friends.

855
00:34:04,957 --> 00:34:06,927
(After washing up cleanly, )

856
00:34:08,127 --> 00:34:09,966
(he changes quickly.)

857
00:34:09,967 --> 00:34:11,727
(Taking out)

858
00:34:14,037 --> 00:34:16,766
(He fills his bag with items.)

859
00:34:16,767 --> 00:34:18,007
It's full.

860
00:34:20,507 --> 00:34:23,077
(He puts on a cap.)

861
00:34:23,947 --> 00:34:28,217
(Then he takes a key and goes outside.)

862
00:34:30,317 --> 00:34:32,047
(Let's go.)

863
00:34:33,557 --> 00:34:34,557
(He arrives at the entrance of the 1st floor.)

864
00:34:34,558 --> 00:34:35,887
(Creaking)

865
00:34:36,857 --> 00:34:38,386
(He comes outside as he listens to the sound...)

866
00:34:38,387 --> 00:34:39,927
(that adds to the atmosphere.)

867
00:34:41,327 --> 00:34:42,527
The neighborhood is pretty.

868
00:34:43,497 --> 00:34:44,567
It's nice.

869
00:34:44,867 --> 00:34:47,737
(Hue's welcomed by the peaceful scenery.)

870
00:34:48,567 --> 00:34:49,637
- It's relaxed. - My goodness.

871
00:34:49,937 --> 00:34:51,007
What's that?

872
00:34:52,737 --> 00:34:56,677
(He walks diligently while carrying a big bag.)

873
00:34:58,607 --> 00:34:59,806
Laundry center?

874
00:34:59,807 --> 00:35:01,017
(He arrives at a laundromat.)

875
00:35:02,347 --> 00:35:04,546
I don't have a washing machine at home,

876
00:35:04,547 --> 00:35:06,517
so I need to go to a coin laundromat.

877
00:35:06,757 --> 00:35:08,286
Firstly, there's no space for a washing machine.

878
00:35:08,287 --> 00:35:10,356
And if I need the landlord's permission...

879
00:35:10,357 --> 00:35:12,457
to put a washing machine in the apartment.

880
00:35:12,727 --> 00:35:14,056
- I see. - But...

881
00:35:14,057 --> 00:35:17,226
since the pipes are too old in this 100-year-old building,

882
00:35:17,227 --> 00:35:19,196
I think it'll be impossible to set up a washing machine...

883
00:35:19,197 --> 00:35:21,297
even if I had space for it.

884
00:35:21,567 --> 00:35:23,866
So I visit a laundromat...

885
00:35:23,867 --> 00:35:25,207
once every 2 to 3 weeks.

886
00:35:25,307 --> 00:35:27,436
In the US, having a washing machine at home...

887
00:35:27,437 --> 00:35:29,476
is a luxury.

888
00:35:29,477 --> 00:35:30,546
- Still? - Yes.

889
00:35:30,547 --> 00:35:33,446
If my friend moves to a new place and has a washing machine,

890
00:35:33,447 --> 00:35:34,716
I say, "You've made it big."

891
00:35:34,717 --> 00:35:36,316
(You've made it big, man.)

892
00:35:36,317 --> 00:35:37,447
How fascinating.

893
00:35:40,487 --> 00:35:41,716
Since he won Tony Awards,

894
00:35:41,717 --> 00:35:43,557
I expected him to live in a luxurious house.

895
00:35:43,927 --> 00:35:46,027
If my parents see this,

896
00:35:46,897 --> 00:35:48,327
they'll find me pathetic.

897
00:35:48,457 --> 00:35:50,126
You won Tony Awards.

898
00:35:50,127 --> 00:35:51,496
You'll get paid now.

899
00:35:51,497 --> 00:35:53,796
- That's right. - You'll make a lot of money now.

900
00:35:53,797 --> 00:35:55,796
- You should rake in the money now. - That's right.

901
00:35:55,797 --> 00:35:56,907
Rake it in.

902
00:35:58,867 --> 00:36:00,807
(Today, he's putting dirty laundry into the washing machine instead.)

903
00:36:03,647 --> 00:36:05,147
(Clinking)

904
00:36:05,607 --> 00:36:07,877
(He puts in the coins that match the price.)

905
00:36:13,017 --> 00:36:15,217
(He opens the top to pour in the laundry detergent.)

906
00:36:16,727 --> 00:36:19,286
I need to go back to the theater in the afternoon,

907
00:36:19,287 --> 00:36:22,297
so I must do everything in the morning.

908
00:36:24,167 --> 00:36:26,897
(Feeling much lighter, he comes out of the laundromat.)

909
00:36:29,937 --> 00:36:32,437
(Checking)

910
00:36:34,307 --> 00:36:36,607
Set a timer for 40 minutes.

911
00:36:38,377 --> 00:36:41,247
You divide up the time very well.

912
00:36:41,317 --> 00:36:43,846
I need to go back and take out my laundry.

913
00:36:43,847 --> 00:36:44,987
That makes sense.

914
00:36:45,187 --> 00:36:48,317
(Shall I walk around while my laundry is getting done?)

915
00:36:52,087 --> 00:36:54,027
- He's entering a restaurant. - Let's eat.

916
00:36:54,527 --> 00:36:56,257
Isn't New York famous for its bagels?

917
00:36:59,567 --> 00:37:02,436
It's a cafe that's near my place.

918
00:37:02,437 --> 00:37:06,506
I actually go there almost every morning.

919
00:37:06,507 --> 00:37:08,406
- They sell bagels. - Bagels sound nice.

920
00:37:08,407 --> 00:37:09,576
Hey, how is it going?

921
00:37:09,577 --> 00:37:11,707
Obviously, having fun. What can I get for you today?

922
00:37:11,807 --> 00:37:15,017
Can I have a BLT on an everything bagel?

923
00:37:15,447 --> 00:37:16,477
What else?

924
00:37:16,787 --> 00:37:19,116
Iced matcha latte with skim milk.

925
00:37:19,117 --> 00:37:21,117
Iced matcha latte with skim milk.

926
00:37:21,457 --> 00:37:24,687
- Is it going to be to stay? - Yes, to stay.

927
00:37:25,127 --> 00:37:26,227
Thank you.

928
00:37:29,397 --> 00:37:31,166
(Hue sits outside.)

929
00:37:31,167 --> 00:37:32,697
How nice.

930
00:37:32,897 --> 00:37:34,266
(Taking out)

931
00:37:34,267 --> 00:37:35,397
It's a book.

932
00:37:35,697 --> 00:37:36,807
You're right.

933
00:37:37,967 --> 00:37:39,466
Even the man behind Hue is reading.

934
00:37:39,467 --> 00:37:41,036
There are many writers in the neighborhood.

935
00:37:41,037 --> 00:37:42,707
It's very cool.

936
00:37:43,277 --> 00:37:45,147
(Writing)

937
00:37:45,747 --> 00:37:49,547
The man behind you must dream of becoming Writer Hue Park.

938
00:37:49,847 --> 00:37:51,687
(Chuckling)

939
00:37:51,847 --> 00:37:53,886
- That's right. - He wouldn't even know who I am.

940
00:37:53,887 --> 00:37:56,516
- Still... - Today, Kai is being a bootlicker.

941
00:37:56,517 --> 00:37:58,187
Gosh, don't say that.

942
00:37:58,327 --> 00:38:00,227
How could you call him a bootlicker?

943
00:38:00,527 --> 00:38:02,456
Why would he dream of becoming Writer Hue Park?

944
00:38:02,457 --> 00:38:03,726
- Think about it. - A bootlicker?

945
00:38:03,727 --> 00:38:06,367
Call him a sweet-talker, okay?

946
00:38:08,337 --> 00:38:11,037
(Kai sweet-talked a lot today.)

947
00:38:12,067 --> 00:38:13,207
(Turning his head)

948
00:38:13,537 --> 00:38:16,207
(Listening right away)

949
00:38:17,307 --> 00:38:20,477
(Kai diligently dreamed of standing next to Hue Park.)

950
00:38:20,617 --> 00:38:23,347
I haven't heard that expression in a long time.

951
00:38:23,617 --> 00:38:27,056
Kai is sweet-talking since he might meet you again.

952
00:38:27,057 --> 00:38:29,086
"I heard that you did musicals in Korea as well."

953
00:38:29,087 --> 00:38:30,586
My goodness.

954
00:38:30,587 --> 00:38:31,727
(Moved)

955
00:38:32,527 --> 00:38:34,497
(There's no sweet-talking in the quiet cafe in New York.)

956
00:38:34,797 --> 00:38:35,897
Here you go.

957
00:38:35,997 --> 00:38:37,427
- Thank you so much. - Enjoy.

958
00:38:39,097 --> 00:38:40,866
(New York's bagel sandwich looks tasty.)

959
00:38:40,867 --> 00:38:41,997
Bagel.

960
00:38:43,507 --> 00:38:44,736
(He picks up a piece...)

961
00:38:44,737 --> 00:38:47,177
(and takes a big bite.)

962
00:38:49,177 --> 00:38:51,107
(He holds the book in place with a cellphone.)

963
00:38:51,547 --> 00:38:53,376
(A book in one hand...)

964
00:38:53,377 --> 00:38:55,647
(and a bagel in the other hand)

965
00:38:56,377 --> 00:38:57,386
(This is how a New Yorker eats.)

966
00:38:57,387 --> 00:38:58,447
It looks delicious.

967
00:39:00,457 --> 00:39:02,156
(He enjoys a cool sip of the bittersweet matcha latte.)

968
00:39:02,157 --> 00:39:04,057
Matcha latte is the best.

969
00:39:04,587 --> 00:39:06,186
Matcha latte must be popular over there as well.

970
00:39:06,187 --> 00:39:07,826
- It's a global trend. - It's a major hit in the US.

971
00:39:07,827 --> 00:39:08,927
Really?

972
00:39:11,997 --> 00:39:14,396
As I eat breakfast at the cafe in the morning,

973
00:39:14,397 --> 00:39:15,737
I read books and write scripts.

974
00:39:15,997 --> 00:39:19,007
Many times, I revised the script of "Maybe Happy Ending" there.

975
00:39:19,607 --> 00:39:22,836
I wrote most of the script of "Il Tenore" there.

976
00:39:22,837 --> 00:39:24,606
I wrote most of the script of "Ghost Bakery" there.

977
00:39:24,607 --> 00:39:27,747
- I wrote many scripts there. - It's a precious place.

978
00:39:28,217 --> 00:39:30,316
It's a meaningful place if I think about it.

979
00:39:30,317 --> 00:39:33,517
It's so nice.

980
00:39:33,787 --> 00:39:35,087
It's so nice.

981
00:39:35,417 --> 00:39:38,586
Kai's words don't sound innocent anymore.

982
00:39:38,587 --> 00:39:40,857
- It's Hyun Moo's fault. - I mean...

983
00:39:41,127 --> 00:39:42,226
It's Hyun Moo's fault.

984
00:39:42,227 --> 00:39:45,496
"I heard that you were working on a new musical."

985
00:39:45,497 --> 00:39:46,596
(There it goes)

986
00:39:46,597 --> 00:39:48,567
There it goes

987
00:39:48,767 --> 00:39:49,896
That's right.

988
00:39:49,897 --> 00:39:51,636
(The sweet-talker even prepared a serenade!)

989
00:39:51,637 --> 00:39:53,167
He even practiced a number.

990
00:39:53,467 --> 00:39:55,976
I'll get goosebumps if Kai is in his musical this autumn.

991
00:39:55,977 --> 00:39:59,207
But he's a great musical actor.

992
00:39:59,407 --> 00:40:02,647
I feel embarrassed before these precious guests.

993
00:40:03,647 --> 00:40:05,717
(This precious guest is reading in a dignified way.)

994
00:40:06,217 --> 00:40:07,687
It's nice.

995
00:40:08,387 --> 00:40:09,986
It's so nice.

996
00:40:09,987 --> 00:40:11,327
(The warm cafe of the day brings inspiration.)

997
00:40:12,527 --> 00:40:16,357
(Hue reads as he enjoys the relaxing time.)

998
00:40:20,367 --> 00:40:22,367
(Getting up)

999
00:40:22,467 --> 00:40:24,967
(I enjoyed the food.)

1000
00:40:26,107 --> 00:40:27,237
Thank you.

1001
00:40:28,407 --> 00:40:30,677
(Walking)

1002
00:40:31,377 --> 00:40:34,447
(He begins to walk with matcha latte in his hand.)

1003
00:40:35,477 --> 00:40:37,447
(Glancing over)

1004
00:40:39,517 --> 00:40:40,786
The streets are so pretty.

1005
00:40:40,787 --> 00:40:41,887
(He observes whatever he sees on the road.)

1006
00:40:42,957 --> 00:40:44,857
(The sweet scenery of the neighborhood)

1007
00:40:47,457 --> 00:40:48,927
I enjoy walking.

1008
00:40:49,227 --> 00:40:50,667
When I walk down the residential area...

1009
00:40:50,927 --> 00:40:52,426
or the streets,

1010
00:40:52,427 --> 00:40:55,896
I become attuned to the rhythm of the city.

1011
00:40:55,897 --> 00:41:00,206
Then I really feel like I'm getting used to the city.

1012
00:41:00,207 --> 00:41:03,607
That's the neighborhood with more houses...

1013
00:41:03,707 --> 00:41:06,047
than apartments.

1014
00:41:06,447 --> 00:41:08,647
The trees are nice.

1015
00:41:11,947 --> 00:41:14,787
(Halting)

1016
00:41:15,517 --> 00:41:17,987
(He suddenly stops and checks the map.)

1017
00:41:19,687 --> 00:41:22,657
(He resumes walking after checking his location.)

1018
00:41:28,037 --> 00:41:29,396
- It's a real estate office. - It's a real estate office.

1019
00:41:29,397 --> 00:41:30,737
It's obvious.

1020
00:41:31,067 --> 00:41:34,006
- They're the same everywhere. - Of course.

1021
00:41:34,007 --> 00:41:36,576
(Advertisements for the properties)

1022
00:41:36,577 --> 00:41:39,446
I began to look for a place.

1023
00:41:39,447 --> 00:41:43,076
"Until when will I be able to carry around my laundry?"

1024
00:41:43,077 --> 00:41:44,116
- I had that thought. - I see.

1025
00:41:44,117 --> 00:41:46,347
- There's no elevator. - That's right.

1026
00:41:46,587 --> 00:41:48,487
I began to dream of buying a house.

1027
00:41:49,987 --> 00:41:52,687
(Where will my house be?)

1028
00:41:55,697 --> 00:41:57,196
(Glancing over)

1029
00:41:57,197 --> 00:41:59,026
Let's look for a property.

1030
00:41:59,027 --> 00:42:01,797
(I'll look them up myself.)

1031
00:42:03,297 --> 00:42:05,207
(Oh, my!)

1032
00:42:05,607 --> 00:42:07,036
- This one is... - How much is it?

1033
00:42:07,037 --> 00:42:08,777
It's 1.22 million dollars.

1034
00:42:09,177 --> 00:42:10,976
Is it an apartment? What is it?

1035
00:42:10,977 --> 00:42:13,507
It's a 2 or 3-story building. It's a house.

1036
00:42:14,377 --> 00:42:15,547
It's sold.

1037
00:42:17,047 --> 00:42:20,086
This one is 1.4 million dollars.

1038
00:42:20,087 --> 00:42:21,356
(The next property is 1.4 million dollars.)

1039
00:42:21,357 --> 00:42:22,957
- It looks nice. - It's sold.

1040
00:42:24,027 --> 00:42:25,157
It has four rooms.

1041
00:42:26,757 --> 00:42:28,697
It isn't like I can buy it.

1042
00:42:29,757 --> 00:42:31,867
(He feels dejected.)

1043
00:42:32,967 --> 00:42:35,267
(Let's look around a bit more.)

1044
00:42:35,367 --> 00:42:38,607
I don't have the means to move right away.

1045
00:42:39,107 --> 00:42:40,977
"If I make a lot of money in the future,"

1046
00:42:41,207 --> 00:42:44,446
"I want to live in a house with many trees." With that thought,

1047
00:42:44,447 --> 00:42:46,877
I check out the houses whenever I go on a stroll.

1048
00:42:47,277 --> 00:42:50,787
I want to plant the trees that need a lot of sunlight.

1049
00:42:51,717 --> 00:42:54,116
In winter, I want to put up the Christmas decorations.

1050
00:42:54,117 --> 00:42:57,257
Since I'm used to living as an international student,

1051
00:42:57,487 --> 00:43:01,157
I've always had an emotional burden of needing...

1052
00:43:01,697 --> 00:43:04,567
to move out of the current place at any time.

1053
00:43:04,797 --> 00:43:07,697
It's the final goal of someone who has lived in this city...

1054
00:43:07,837 --> 00:43:11,537
for many years as an international student.

1055
00:43:13,107 --> 00:43:14,737
You should visit "Where Is My Home."

1056
00:43:15,337 --> 00:43:17,577
How about our special on filial duty?

1057
00:43:17,977 --> 00:43:20,007
- Finding a house? - A special on filial duty.

1058
00:43:20,247 --> 00:43:22,217
If I send my mom there, I'll be an unfilial son.

1059
00:43:23,347 --> 00:43:25,647
Imagine sending your mom to New York to be a filial son.

1060
00:43:26,117 --> 00:43:27,957
My mom should try living in a nice city.

1061
00:43:28,217 --> 00:43:30,887
How will she live there with the language barrier?

1062
00:43:31,687 --> 00:43:32,956
In the center of the world...

1063
00:43:32,957 --> 00:43:34,526
She should try a bagel.

1064
00:43:34,527 --> 00:43:35,797
(Mom, Moomoo will let you eat a bagel!)

1065
00:43:37,297 --> 00:43:38,997
When will I be able to live in a house like that?

1066
00:43:40,697 --> 00:43:41,767
Bye.

1067
00:43:46,267 --> 00:43:49,137
(After checking out some houses, he picks up his laundry.)

1068
00:43:49,907 --> 00:43:52,677
Since I just came back from checking out the houses,

1069
00:43:53,077 --> 00:43:54,276
I couldn't help but think,

1070
00:43:54,277 --> 00:43:56,316
"Until when will I need to use a laundromat?"

1071
00:43:56,317 --> 00:43:57,446
You had a reality check.

1072
00:43:57,447 --> 00:43:58,447
(Sad)

1073
00:44:00,517 --> 00:44:03,927
(He goes up the stairs with a heavy bag.)

1074
00:44:05,287 --> 00:44:06,287
(Sighing)

1075
00:44:11,767 --> 00:44:14,497
(Pouring out)

1076
00:44:15,697 --> 00:44:18,637
(He folds the clean clothes as soon as he comes home.)

1077
00:44:21,237 --> 00:44:24,607
(He puts them in the drawers right away.)

1078
00:44:26,377 --> 00:44:27,617
(Ringing)

1079
00:44:29,117 --> 00:44:30,746
(Who is ringing the doorbell?)

1080
00:44:30,747 --> 00:44:32,386
(Curious)

1081
00:44:32,387 --> 00:44:33,686
Is it a guest? Is it a friend?

1082
00:44:33,687 --> 00:44:35,386
- Hello. - Will.

1083
00:44:35,387 --> 00:44:36,487
- Will? - Hello.

1084
00:44:36,787 --> 00:44:37,887
Hello.

1085
00:44:38,227 --> 00:44:41,057
He's a writer and a composer, Will Aronson.

1086
00:44:42,627 --> 00:44:45,397
He majored in music at Harvard University...

1087
00:44:46,067 --> 00:44:48,397
and studied musical theater at New York University.

1088
00:44:49,337 --> 00:44:53,767
He's a friend I've been working with for 14 years.

1089
00:44:54,137 --> 00:44:55,976
("Maybe Happy Ending," "Bungee Jumping of Their Own,")

1090
00:44:55,977 --> 00:44:58,007
("Il Tenore," "Ghost Bakery")

1091
00:44:58,107 --> 00:45:00,817
You get a set amount of time to say the acceptance speech.

1092
00:45:01,217 --> 00:45:03,517
They only give you 1 minute and 30 seconds.

1093
00:45:03,677 --> 00:45:05,516
- There's a time limit. - Yes.

1094
00:45:05,517 --> 00:45:07,616
So many people misunderstand. Because we're always together,

1095
00:45:07,617 --> 00:45:10,087
people just assume that we're a couple.

1096
00:45:10,557 --> 00:45:12,257
- It's New York. - It's New York.

1097
00:45:12,627 --> 00:45:13,956
Is he married?

1098
00:45:13,957 --> 00:45:16,227
Yes. Will is happily married.

1099
00:45:16,697 --> 00:45:18,267
I felt so victimized...

1100
00:45:18,367 --> 00:45:20,636
that I revealed it as a part of the acceptance speech.

1101
00:45:20,637 --> 00:45:21,997
(He explained himself during the acceptance speech.)

1102
00:45:22,337 --> 00:45:24,466
I had just moved to New York...

1103
00:45:24,467 --> 00:45:28,137
and Will had come back after composing a musical in Korea.

1104
00:45:28,607 --> 00:45:31,076
There was a mutual friend of ours.

1105
00:45:31,077 --> 00:45:33,446
"You wrote lyrics in Korea."

1106
00:45:33,447 --> 00:45:35,376
"He's a composer. He composed numbers in Korea."

1107
00:45:35,377 --> 00:45:37,386
"Shouldn't the two of you get to know each other?"

1108
00:45:37,387 --> 00:45:39,647
That was how we became friends.

1109
00:45:40,217 --> 00:45:41,716
It was fate.

1110
00:45:41,717 --> 00:45:43,657
(Will and Hue remain best friends and partners.)

1111
00:45:43,857 --> 00:45:45,586
I don't know why, but...

1112
00:45:45,587 --> 00:45:47,797
- "Will and Hue" go well together. - Right.

1113
00:45:47,857 --> 00:45:49,756
"Hue and Will" sounds weird. It's better the other way around.

1114
00:45:49,757 --> 00:45:50,996
"Will and Hue" go well together.

1115
00:45:50,997 --> 00:45:52,096
They go well together.

1116
00:45:52,097 --> 00:45:53,867
They go well together like Dolce and Gabbana.

1117
00:45:54,437 --> 00:45:56,097
- Are you joining in as well? - Hold on.

1118
00:45:56,167 --> 00:45:58,266
Do you want to be in musicals too?

1119
00:45:58,267 --> 00:45:59,267
(The new sweet-talker does his best.)

1120
00:45:59,967 --> 00:46:01,677
I have a special delivery.

1121
00:46:02,407 --> 00:46:03,607
What is it?

1122
00:46:03,737 --> 00:46:06,046
I'm not sure if you would know.

1123
00:46:06,047 --> 00:46:07,716
Where can I bring it?

1124
00:46:07,717 --> 00:46:08,947
We can go to the living room.

1125
00:46:11,247 --> 00:46:12,516
(He follows the Korean way and naturally takes off his shoes.)

1126
00:46:12,517 --> 00:46:14,757
Of course, he must follow the Korean way.

1127
00:46:18,687 --> 00:46:20,327
- Are you ready? - Yes.

1128
00:46:20,757 --> 00:46:22,097
Hold on.

1129
00:46:22,727 --> 00:46:25,197
He brought that up five floors.

1130
00:46:25,567 --> 00:46:26,627
What could it be?

1131
00:46:27,937 --> 00:46:28,937
It's food.

1132
00:46:28,938 --> 00:46:30,066
- No. - Oh, my gosh!

1133
00:46:30,067 --> 00:46:31,607
- "Tony?" - It's the Tony Award.

1134
00:46:31,737 --> 00:46:33,107
She just called that food.

1135
00:46:34,837 --> 00:46:35,876
(Smiling from ear to ear)

1136
00:46:35,877 --> 00:46:37,877
Oh, my gosh.

1137
00:46:38,147 --> 00:46:39,377
It's so heavy.

1138
00:46:40,817 --> 00:46:43,847
The Tony Award trophy...

1139
00:46:43,917 --> 00:46:45,987
had to have my name carved into it.

1140
00:46:46,147 --> 00:46:48,216
I sent it back to the organizer.

1141
00:46:48,217 --> 00:46:51,856
The trophy was ready and I had to go back to pick it up,

1142
00:46:51,857 --> 00:46:53,287
but I was in Seoul.

1143
00:46:53,627 --> 00:46:56,657
Will went for me to pick it up...

1144
00:46:56,727 --> 00:46:59,996
and he finally brought it to my house.

1145
00:46:59,997 --> 00:47:01,366
Was the trophy given to each person?

1146
00:47:01,367 --> 00:47:02,496
Yes.

1147
00:47:02,497 --> 00:47:04,107
- I see. - That's nice.

1148
00:47:04,437 --> 00:47:06,367
Gosh, it's already been two months.

1149
00:47:07,877 --> 00:47:09,037
Oh, my gosh!

1150
00:47:09,907 --> 00:47:12,207
(He opens it right away.)

1151
00:47:14,047 --> 00:47:15,547
- Gosh! - Gosh!

1152
00:47:15,617 --> 00:47:16,646
- Gosh! - How was it?

1153
00:47:16,647 --> 00:47:17,687
That's impressive.

1154
00:47:17,787 --> 00:47:19,146
It felt weird...

1155
00:47:19,147 --> 00:47:20,717
to see my name on it.

1156
00:47:21,987 --> 00:47:23,057
Amazing.

1157
00:47:23,157 --> 00:47:24,556
You've got two more, so you know,

1158
00:47:24,557 --> 00:47:26,426
don't spend too much time.

1159
00:47:26,427 --> 00:47:27,826
(Joking)

1160
00:47:27,827 --> 00:47:29,326
Here's number two.

1161
00:47:29,327 --> 00:47:30,826
You're going to go through all of them?

1162
00:47:30,827 --> 00:47:32,867
- I guess so. - That's truly amazing.

1163
00:47:35,737 --> 00:47:38,737
- Really nice, isn't it? - Best Original Score.

1164
00:47:40,707 --> 00:47:41,777
The last one.

1165
00:47:44,207 --> 00:47:46,877
Best Book of a Musical.

1166
00:47:48,277 --> 00:47:49,577
I'm getting emotional.

1167
00:47:50,117 --> 00:47:51,416
Super emotional.

1168
00:47:51,417 --> 00:47:53,147
(Laughing)

1169
00:47:53,187 --> 00:47:54,957
Best Book of a Musical.

1170
00:47:55,757 --> 00:47:56,917
Best Score.

1171
00:47:57,327 --> 00:47:58,686
- Gosh. - Gosh.

1172
00:47:58,687 --> 00:48:00,026
This is for the Best Musical.

1173
00:48:00,027 --> 00:48:01,357
That's amazing.

1174
00:48:01,827 --> 00:48:02,897
It's insane.

1175
00:48:03,657 --> 00:48:06,167
Shouldn't they be put into a glass display case?

1176
00:48:06,297 --> 00:48:08,467
Kai is about to send one for him.

1177
00:48:09,097 --> 00:48:10,567
If only that's possible...

1178
00:48:11,037 --> 00:48:13,006
He would send a crystal one.

1179
00:48:13,007 --> 00:48:14,976
Would a glass one be enough? It should be a crystal one.

1180
00:48:14,977 --> 00:48:16,276
- "Made by Kai." - Right.

1181
00:48:16,277 --> 00:48:18,147
Of course.

1182
00:48:18,247 --> 00:48:20,106
Yes. Because I think you're going to have these for a long time.

1183
00:48:20,107 --> 00:48:22,317
If you ever move apartments, you're going to need these to...

1184
00:48:22,347 --> 00:48:24,247
Should I keep these wrappers too?

1185
00:48:24,517 --> 00:48:25,887
You have to keep the plastic bags too.

1186
00:48:25,987 --> 00:48:27,286
- Of course. - I would keep them.

1187
00:48:27,287 --> 00:48:29,017
- I would keep them too. - I would keep them too.

1188
00:48:29,087 --> 00:48:30,686
I don't want to throw it out yet.

1189
00:48:30,687 --> 00:48:32,757
I'm going to put this in the box.

1190
00:48:32,927 --> 00:48:34,097
Okay. Sounds good.

1191
00:48:35,327 --> 00:48:37,427
- I couldn't throw it out easily. - Right.

1192
00:48:37,997 --> 00:48:41,597
I got some stuff from H-Mart. I don't know if you want it.

1193
00:48:41,897 --> 00:48:42,997
Let's take a look.

1194
00:48:44,537 --> 00:48:46,707
Well, I got...

1195
00:48:46,867 --> 00:48:48,707
Gosh, there's barely anything inside.

1196
00:48:48,807 --> 00:48:49,837
Do you not cook at all?

1197
00:48:50,237 --> 00:48:51,506
There are times when I do and I don't.

1198
00:48:51,507 --> 00:48:54,277
I haven't been cooking these days.

1199
00:48:55,117 --> 00:48:56,176
- I got this. - You should feel comfortable...

1200
00:48:56,177 --> 00:48:57,246
- Sushi. - to eat it.

1201
00:48:57,247 --> 00:48:59,317
I can sit here and have a glass of water.

1202
00:48:59,787 --> 00:49:00,787
And...

1203
00:49:01,517 --> 00:49:03,316
Ta-da! It's champagne.

1204
00:49:03,317 --> 00:49:04,756
- Champagne. - Are we having some of this?

1205
00:49:04,757 --> 00:49:05,856
- Yes. - Are you showing it to me?

1206
00:49:05,857 --> 00:49:06,956
I've been saving it.

1207
00:49:06,957 --> 00:49:09,397
- Oh, great. - To celebrate.

1208
00:49:09,557 --> 00:49:10,626
Okay. I can't wait.

1209
00:49:10,627 --> 00:49:11,627
Can I help? What do I do?

1210
00:49:12,227 --> 00:49:14,067
No. I'll take care of it.

1211
00:49:15,397 --> 00:49:17,966
Gosh, that's so cute.

1212
00:49:17,967 --> 00:49:19,507
- What? - That surprised me.

1213
00:49:19,937 --> 00:49:21,276
I've never seen that.

1214
00:49:21,277 --> 00:49:22,407
- What? - What?

1215
00:49:22,537 --> 00:49:24,446
- Should it go back in the fridge? - I'm getting goosebumps.

1216
00:49:24,447 --> 00:49:25,546
- What? - His Korean is so good.

1217
00:49:25,547 --> 00:49:27,177
This is scarier than "The Sixth Sense."

1218
00:49:27,747 --> 00:49:29,447
Will's Korean...

1219
00:49:29,817 --> 00:49:32,616
is improving day by day at a scary rate.

1220
00:49:32,617 --> 00:49:33,887
He's from Harvard.

1221
00:49:34,357 --> 00:49:36,916
(I loved you)

1222
00:49:36,917 --> 00:49:38,827
- "Southbound Train" by Kim Su Hee? - It's "Southbound Train."

1223
00:49:39,927 --> 00:49:41,327
He has Harvard on his face.

1224
00:49:41,657 --> 00:49:42,856
Stop speaking nonsense.

1225
00:49:42,857 --> 00:49:44,126
You can see pencils on his face.

1226
00:49:44,127 --> 00:49:45,467
What are you saying?

1227
00:49:45,797 --> 00:49:48,037
Would you like to take this first?

1228
00:49:48,237 --> 00:49:49,237
Okay.

1229
00:49:49,937 --> 00:49:51,366
Just take this first.

1230
00:49:51,367 --> 00:49:53,937
(The man that reminds you of pencils moves the food.)

1231
00:49:56,407 --> 00:49:57,947
(The wine glasses are placed too.)

1232
00:49:59,047 --> 00:50:00,447
Multi-grain rice...

1233
00:50:01,317 --> 00:50:02,347
and braised black beans?

1234
00:50:03,547 --> 00:50:04,616
(The celebration meal for the two is ready.)

1235
00:50:04,617 --> 00:50:05,917
Gosh, they're having rice and champagne.

1236
00:50:06,487 --> 00:50:07,517
All right.

1237
00:50:07,557 --> 00:50:09,287
- Congratulations. - Congratulations.

1238
00:50:10,417 --> 00:50:11,427
We made it.

1239
00:50:11,657 --> 00:50:13,556
(A sip in honor of the Tony Award)

1240
00:50:13,557 --> 00:50:15,227
The two must feel delighted.

1241
00:50:16,927 --> 00:50:18,997
I want some rice,

1242
00:50:19,397 --> 00:50:20,727
so you should have this.

1243
00:50:21,397 --> 00:50:23,567
(Hue moves the sushi closer to Will.)

1244
00:50:23,897 --> 00:50:27,106
Won't it be awkward to eat the sushi with a fork?

1245
00:50:27,107 --> 00:50:29,237
(There's only one pair of chopsticks.)

1246
00:50:29,337 --> 00:50:31,276
You should use these then.

1247
00:50:31,277 --> 00:50:32,746
- I only have 1 pair. - You only have 1 pair?

1248
00:50:32,747 --> 00:50:35,147
- Right. - You must really live alone.

1249
00:50:36,417 --> 00:50:38,747
- His Korean is so good. - His Korean is so good.

1250
00:50:38,847 --> 00:50:40,517
Did you have to poke at me where it hurts?

1251
00:50:41,017 --> 00:50:43,257
- I'm sorry. - Gosh.

1252
00:50:43,287 --> 00:50:45,486
Does it feel weird to eat sushi with a fork?

1253
00:50:45,487 --> 00:50:47,227
Right. It's a bit strange.

1254
00:50:47,727 --> 00:50:49,057
You've become an Asian now.

1255
00:50:50,627 --> 00:50:52,597
So he uses chopsticks.

1256
00:50:53,127 --> 00:50:54,667
He's good at using them.

1257
00:50:55,367 --> 00:50:57,606
Should we sit on the floor?

1258
00:50:57,607 --> 00:50:58,607
Sure.

1259
00:50:59,007 --> 00:51:00,066
He does that too?

1260
00:51:00,067 --> 00:51:01,437
(Plopping down naturally)

1261
00:51:01,477 --> 00:51:04,446
- That's a true Korean. - That's a true Korean.

1262
00:51:04,447 --> 00:51:06,607
He's certainly a South Korean foreigner.

1263
00:51:07,477 --> 00:51:09,517
Only a Korean would sit on the floor against the couch.

1264
00:51:13,747 --> 00:51:16,316
Hue is eating with the fork instead.

1265
00:51:16,317 --> 00:51:17,986
I already insisted first that...

1266
00:51:17,987 --> 00:51:19,727
sushi must be eaten with chopsticks.

1267
00:51:20,187 --> 00:51:21,297
It's really good.

1268
00:51:21,757 --> 00:51:22,857
I'm not just being polite.

1269
00:51:24,267 --> 00:51:27,127
- That's a relief. - Where find this sushi?

1270
00:51:27,637 --> 00:51:29,637
Did you mean, "Where did you find this sushi?"

1271
00:51:29,767 --> 00:51:31,436
Right. What did I just say?

1272
00:51:31,437 --> 00:51:33,306
"Where find this sushi?"

1273
00:51:33,307 --> 00:51:34,677
Where find this sushi?

1274
00:51:35,737 --> 00:51:36,877
I must be crazy.

1275
00:51:38,647 --> 00:51:40,947
- He's surely a Korean. - Gosh, that's hilarious.

1276
00:51:42,047 --> 00:51:44,917
We understand each other even without saying anything.

1277
00:51:45,017 --> 00:51:46,716
We can all tell if we are upset.

1278
00:51:46,717 --> 00:51:48,056
We can tell if we are happy as well.

1279
00:51:48,057 --> 00:51:49,387
We feel like twins.

1280
00:51:51,527 --> 00:51:53,526
(The two can tell just by looking each other in the eye.)

1281
00:51:53,527 --> 00:51:54,626
It's good.

1282
00:51:54,627 --> 00:51:56,257
- Really? - It's different but it's good.

1283
00:51:57,897 --> 00:52:02,137
I like multi-grain rice.

1284
00:52:02,367 --> 00:52:03,837
(Groaning)

1285
00:52:03,967 --> 00:52:05,937
It's too healthy,

1286
00:52:06,867 --> 00:52:08,277
so it doesn't taste that good.

1287
00:52:08,507 --> 00:52:10,337
I have the taste of a middle-aged man.

1288
00:52:11,777 --> 00:52:13,847
I hated beans when I was young.

1289
00:52:14,377 --> 00:52:16,077
- What about now? - I like them.

1290
00:52:16,317 --> 00:52:17,447
(Laughing)

1291
00:52:17,847 --> 00:52:19,386
You have definitely become a middle-aged man.

1292
00:52:19,387 --> 00:52:21,017
He's teasing him in Korean.

1293
00:52:21,287 --> 00:52:23,157
We always tease each other.

1294
00:52:24,587 --> 00:52:27,026
(The middle-aged man persistently eats the beans and rice.)

1295
00:52:27,027 --> 00:52:28,397
(Eating)

1296
00:52:29,157 --> 00:52:30,996
(Will eats the watermelon.)

1297
00:52:30,997 --> 00:52:31,997
It's good.

1298
00:52:33,397 --> 00:52:35,567
He's fully equipped with Korean.

1299
00:52:38,037 --> 00:52:39,637
He must've been really hungry.

1300
00:52:39,907 --> 00:52:41,476
I said that I wasn't hungry.

1301
00:52:41,477 --> 00:52:44,277
I said that in case...

1302
00:52:45,107 --> 00:52:47,206
- you didn't have any food. - It was a white lie?

1303
00:52:47,207 --> 00:52:49,176
Yes. Actually, I was very hungry.

1304
00:52:49,177 --> 00:52:51,447
- He's so cute. - He's so cute.

1305
00:52:51,647 --> 00:52:54,486
I didn't know if you had food or not,

1306
00:52:54,487 --> 00:52:55,757
so I just lied.

1307
00:52:56,887 --> 00:52:58,826
- Were you being considerate? - Yes.

1308
00:52:58,827 --> 00:53:00,457
In case he felt awkward if there wasn't any food.

1309
00:53:00,487 --> 00:53:02,557
(A great meal thanks to each other's consideration)

1310
00:53:04,827 --> 00:53:07,467
We clinked our glasses for the finale.

1311
00:53:08,237 --> 00:53:10,466
I think I was very fortunate.

1312
00:53:10,467 --> 00:53:12,466
I came to New York...

1313
00:53:12,467 --> 00:53:14,837
and went through a lot of special experiences.

1314
00:53:15,477 --> 00:53:17,947
There were many tough times as a foreign student,

1315
00:53:18,247 --> 00:53:20,307
but I also enjoyed my time working here.

1316
00:53:20,407 --> 00:53:23,246
The musical that I wrote as a writer...

1317
00:53:23,247 --> 00:53:25,786
is being shown to the audience in Broadway...

1318
00:53:25,787 --> 00:53:27,917
every single day.

1319
00:53:27,987 --> 00:53:29,756
It still feels like a dream.

1320
00:53:29,757 --> 00:53:31,657
It does feel like a movie.

1321
00:53:32,157 --> 00:53:34,856
I always think that...

1322
00:53:34,857 --> 00:53:36,326
I'm living a fortunate life.

1323
00:53:36,327 --> 00:53:37,827
(I might be a very lucky person.)

1324
00:53:38,827 --> 00:53:41,437
Let's congratulate each other one last time. Let's make a toast,

1325
00:53:41,697 --> 00:53:43,366
- cheers, and clink! - Cheers and clink!

1326
00:53:43,367 --> 00:53:45,607
That's what you said. Yes.

1327
00:53:49,207 --> 00:53:51,147
- Thanks for... - I should pack up.

1328
00:53:51,577 --> 00:53:53,347
Bringing all the trophies.

1329
00:53:54,577 --> 00:53:56,617
All right. Well, congratulations.

1330
00:53:56,747 --> 00:53:57,847
Congrats again!

1331
00:53:57,987 --> 00:53:59,746
Although the work continues.

1332
00:53:59,747 --> 00:54:00,847
I know.

1333
00:54:03,057 --> 00:54:04,217
So I'll see you at the theater.

1334
00:54:04,357 --> 00:54:05,787
- Yes. Bye, Will! - Bye.

1335
00:54:06,887 --> 00:54:09,097
(He cleans up after seeing Will off.)

1336
00:54:09,327 --> 00:54:10,357
Alexa.

1337
00:54:10,957 --> 00:54:13,327
Play "Perfect Places" by Lorde.

1338
00:54:15,037 --> 00:54:18,467
(Wiping)

1339
00:54:19,367 --> 00:54:22,507
(He finishes up quickly while listening to music.)

1340
00:54:24,747 --> 00:54:26,907
(Picking up)

1341
00:54:29,747 --> 00:54:31,046
(He continues to get to work.)

1342
00:54:31,047 --> 00:54:32,647
Oh, right. He has to get to work.

1343
00:54:33,217 --> 00:54:36,617
I want to continue to improve.

1344
00:54:36,957 --> 00:54:38,587
New York to me...

1345
00:54:39,187 --> 00:54:41,426
is part of my identity...

1346
00:54:41,427 --> 00:54:44,197
and a place of love and hate.

1347
00:54:44,357 --> 00:54:46,366
There's a certain energy that this place gives...

1348
00:54:46,367 --> 00:54:48,637
and that formed who I am now.

1349
00:54:49,037 --> 00:54:51,337
Living alone...

1350
00:54:51,707 --> 00:54:55,037
could feel lonely or exciting.

1351
00:54:55,177 --> 00:54:58,907
I want it to be a happy ending for me.

1352
00:54:59,007 --> 00:55:01,417
A life that I want to continue to develop.

1353
00:55:01,677 --> 00:55:03,517
That is the life that I live alone.

1354
00:55:03,647 --> 00:55:07,017
(My life that dreams of happiness and improvement)

1355
00:55:08,017 --> 00:55:09,057
Alexa.

1356
00:55:09,887 --> 00:55:11,187
Turn off all the lights.

1357
00:55:21,937 --> 00:55:23,166
(Certainly Happy Ending)

1358
00:55:23,167 --> 00:55:24,906
- That's impressive. - That's impressive.

1359
00:55:24,907 --> 00:55:26,007
That's truly impressive.

1360
00:55:26,707 --> 00:55:28,606
I feel a bit embarrassed.

1361
00:55:28,607 --> 00:55:29,836
- Why? - Why feel embarrassed?

1362
00:55:29,837 --> 00:55:30,936
- You haven't been writing. - No.

1363
00:55:30,937 --> 00:55:32,346
- To be honest, - You follow your own thoughts.

1364
00:55:32,347 --> 00:55:34,176
- Right. - after making a lot of webtoons,

1365
00:55:34,177 --> 00:55:35,817
I fell into this mannerism.

1366
00:55:36,117 --> 00:55:38,447
The passion that I had at start withered away.

1367
00:55:38,487 --> 00:55:39,946
But he wasn't like that.

1368
00:55:39,947 --> 00:55:41,586
He still cries after watching his own work.

1369
00:55:41,587 --> 00:55:43,586
Right. That was the most impressive part to me too.

1370
00:55:43,587 --> 00:55:47,387
Should I say that he looked bubbly?

1371
00:55:47,487 --> 00:55:50,357
I was jealous of how he looked innocent.

1372
00:55:51,167 --> 00:55:52,527
- That was what I felt. - Thank you.

1373
00:55:52,667 --> 00:55:56,867
I mentioned him before. Around 14 years ago,

1374
00:55:56,997 --> 00:55:58,267
I was with my NYX.

1375
00:55:58,367 --> 00:56:00,007
My ex that was in New York.

1376
00:56:00,267 --> 00:56:01,406
- Oh, "NYX." - I see.

1377
00:56:01,407 --> 00:56:02,736
I was with my NYX before.

1378
00:56:02,737 --> 00:56:04,706
When I first went,

1379
00:56:04,707 --> 00:56:06,676
we went to watch a musical in Times Square.

1380
00:56:06,677 --> 00:56:09,377
- What did you watch? - We watched "Chicago."

1381
00:56:09,777 --> 00:56:11,546
But I didn't speak any English at all.

1382
00:56:11,547 --> 00:56:14,817
Everyone else laughed, but I didn't understand why.

1383
00:56:15,057 --> 00:56:17,657
I remember when I laughed when the others laughed.

1384
00:56:18,127 --> 00:56:20,857
At the age of 27,

1385
00:56:21,157 --> 00:56:22,796
I imagined what it would be like...

1386
00:56:22,797 --> 00:56:24,766
to meet a person who makes these musicals.

1387
00:56:24,767 --> 00:56:26,966
I thought that to myself when I couldn't speak any English.

1388
00:56:26,967 --> 00:56:28,766
But as he's right here next to me,

1389
00:56:28,767 --> 00:56:31,406
- it feels really strange for me. - Right.

1390
00:56:31,407 --> 00:56:32,867
- It must feel strange. - Right.

1391
00:56:33,337 --> 00:56:35,137
I was really impressed.

1392
00:56:36,107 --> 00:56:37,706
What did you think, Kai?

1393
00:56:37,707 --> 00:56:39,947
(What did you think, Kai?)

1394
00:56:40,077 --> 00:56:42,276
Do you think you have achieved your goal?

1395
00:56:42,277 --> 00:56:43,277
(Joking)

1396
00:56:43,278 --> 00:56:44,986
If you don't feel satisfied,

1397
00:56:44,987 --> 00:56:46,586
- feel free to speak. - Right.

1398
00:56:46,587 --> 00:56:48,356
We should let him sing.

1399
00:56:48,357 --> 00:56:49,416
We should hold an audition.

1400
00:56:49,417 --> 00:56:52,256
It's true that I love Will and Hue's musical.

1401
00:56:52,257 --> 00:56:53,257
Right.

1402
00:56:53,258 --> 00:56:57,656
I'm not sure. It made me reflect upon myself as well.

1403
00:56:57,657 --> 00:57:00,327
After being an actor for around 20 years,

1404
00:57:00,867 --> 00:57:03,296
I naturally fell into a habitual routine.

1405
00:57:03,297 --> 00:57:04,367
- Right. - Right.

1406
00:57:04,467 --> 00:57:08,077
After watching Hue's day,

1407
00:57:08,277 --> 00:57:10,237
I wondered if I was really doing well.

1408
00:57:10,637 --> 00:57:14,676
I wondered if I didn't lose those pure and innocent moments.

1409
00:57:14,677 --> 00:57:17,476
I felt a bit of regret based on those thoughts.

1410
00:57:17,477 --> 00:57:20,287
I really enjoyed watching how he lived his life.

1411
00:57:20,387 --> 00:57:22,487
I am excited to see what he'll do in the future as well.

1412
00:57:22,617 --> 00:57:24,216
It's like you're writing a biography of him.

1413
00:57:24,217 --> 00:57:26,556
(It's like you're writing a biography of him.)

1414
00:57:26,557 --> 00:57:27,586
(Laughing)

1415
00:57:27,587 --> 00:57:28,996
Was it not a lecture?

1416
00:57:28,997 --> 00:57:30,056
- No. - No?

1417
00:57:30,057 --> 00:57:31,326
We should each get ready for an audition.

1418
00:57:31,327 --> 00:57:32,666
My gosh.

1419
00:57:32,667 --> 00:57:33,967
(Singing)

1420
00:57:34,837 --> 00:57:36,667
(I'll go through that audition now.)

1421
00:57:37,637 --> 00:57:39,167
(It's an honor.)

1422
00:57:39,437 --> 00:57:40,836
There will be an opportunity to meet each other.

1423
00:57:40,837 --> 00:57:41,936
- Right. - Definitely.

1424
00:57:41,937 --> 00:57:43,836
We hope that your life will become a happy ending.

1425
00:57:43,837 --> 00:57:44,946
We will root for you.

1426
00:57:44,947 --> 00:57:46,377
(We root for Hue Park's happy ending.)

1427
00:57:47,247 --> 00:57:48,317
(I Live Alone)

1428
00:57:48,977 --> 00:57:52,047
(A Miraculous Day)

1429
00:57:53,187 --> 00:57:55,156
(In the dark room, )

1430
00:57:55,157 --> 00:57:57,217
(Kian84 is fast asleep.)

1431
00:57:58,027 --> 00:57:59,226
- Where is this? - Is that his house or what?

1432
00:57:59,227 --> 00:58:00,326
- It's not his house. - It's not his house.

1433
00:58:00,327 --> 00:58:01,396
- It's not his house. - It is my house.

1434
00:58:01,397 --> 00:58:02,627
- That's your house? - Yes.

1435
00:58:02,657 --> 00:58:04,667
- I thought he was at a motel. - Same here.

1436
00:58:06,097 --> 00:58:10,037
(He's sleeping at home.)

1437
00:58:11,367 --> 00:58:12,636
(He starts wriggling at the sound of the alarm.)

1438
00:58:12,637 --> 00:58:13,737
It's that sound.

1439
00:58:15,577 --> 00:58:17,477
(Turning off the alarm)

1440
00:58:18,107 --> 00:58:20,377
(He gets up after letting out a sigh.)

1441
00:58:21,817 --> 00:58:23,046
What? It's 4am?

1442
00:58:23,047 --> 00:58:24,277
- Why get up so early? - Gosh.

1443
00:58:25,617 --> 00:58:28,387
I wake up from time to time at that time.

1444
00:58:28,587 --> 00:58:31,326
I had something planned at 5am that day.

1445
00:58:31,327 --> 00:58:32,926
I usually sleep at around that time.

1446
00:58:32,927 --> 00:58:35,497
If I train myself to wake up earlier,

1447
00:58:35,727 --> 00:58:38,227
there might be a time when I am able to spend longer days.

1448
00:58:38,497 --> 00:58:41,337
That was why I decided to wake up early.

1449
00:58:45,367 --> 00:58:47,707
- I feel tired. - That's really early though.

1450
00:58:49,307 --> 00:58:50,876
(He drags out his tired body out of the bed...)

1451
00:58:50,877 --> 00:58:53,777
(although it's really early.)

1452
00:58:56,777 --> 00:58:57,777
(Sighing)

1453
00:58:59,487 --> 00:59:01,017
(Opening)

1454
00:59:03,117 --> 00:59:04,357
(Coffee)

1455
00:59:06,487 --> 00:59:09,097
(Pouring the coffee into the tumbler)

1456
00:59:11,927 --> 00:59:12,927
(Taking a sip of morning coffee)

1457
00:59:12,928 --> 00:59:14,626
You must have coffee at that time.

1458
00:59:14,627 --> 00:59:15,837
It was hard to stay awake.

1459
00:59:19,167 --> 00:59:21,437
(He places the tumbler next to his things.)

1460
00:59:27,647 --> 00:59:29,646
(Loud blast)

1461
00:59:29,647 --> 00:59:31,416
- I blew my nose. - Your nose makes that sound?

1462
00:59:31,417 --> 00:59:32,687
(I blew my nose.)

1463
00:59:33,917 --> 00:59:36,687
(Taking a refreshing shower at 4:04am)

1464
00:59:40,087 --> 00:59:41,656
(Picking up)

1465
00:59:41,657 --> 00:59:43,027
He picked up his helmet.

1466
00:59:44,427 --> 00:59:46,297
(Putting on his hat)

1467
00:59:46,827 --> 00:59:48,837
(Kian84 is ready to leave.)

1468
00:59:50,437 --> 00:59:51,807
He doesn't look bright at all.

1469
00:59:51,907 --> 00:59:53,767
It's a time when it's hard to look bright.

1470
00:59:55,977 --> 00:59:58,077
(Shall I get going now?)

1471
00:59:58,977 --> 01:00:01,277
(Leaving at 4:19am)

1472
01:00:03,577 --> 01:00:05,617
Look at that. The sun isn't even out yet.

1473
01:00:06,517 --> 01:00:08,757
(The streets are silent without the sun out.)

1474
01:00:10,017 --> 01:00:11,156
(He starts the day at dawn on his own.)

1475
01:00:11,157 --> 01:00:12,886
Isn't that before the first bus is out?

1476
01:00:12,887 --> 01:00:14,357
Right. There are no buses at that time.

1477
01:00:15,497 --> 01:00:16,497
(Lifting)

1478
01:00:17,497 --> 01:00:19,026
Taxis must be taken during those times.

1479
01:00:19,027 --> 01:00:20,396
But why did you bring out your helmet?

1480
01:00:20,397 --> 01:00:22,936
I was going to use the shared bikes...

1481
01:00:22,937 --> 01:00:24,807
or electric scooters.

1482
01:00:25,267 --> 01:00:27,806
I only used taxis as it was really hot that day.

1483
01:00:27,807 --> 01:00:29,006
It is really hot.

1484
01:00:29,007 --> 01:00:30,637
I shouldn't have brought that out.

1485
01:00:31,577 --> 01:00:33,507
(Moving with the taxi while escaping from the heat)

1486
01:00:36,017 --> 01:00:39,047
(He dozes off while going across the bridge.)

1487
01:00:40,647 --> 01:00:42,187
I'll get off here.

1488
01:00:42,557 --> 01:00:43,687
Thank you.

1489
01:00:47,327 --> 01:00:49,027
- Gosh, it's hot. - Where is this?

1490
01:00:52,197 --> 01:00:54,497
(Han River Park?)

1491
01:00:55,267 --> 01:00:57,196
- Hello. - Hello.

1492
01:00:57,197 --> 01:00:58,637
Hello.

1493
01:00:58,837 --> 01:01:00,166
It's my first time seeing Kian84.

1494
01:01:00,167 --> 01:01:01,777
It's my first time running with him.

1495
01:01:02,107 --> 01:01:03,606
What I had planned at 5am...

1496
01:01:03,607 --> 01:01:07,677
was to run by the Han River with others.

1497
01:01:08,577 --> 01:01:10,776
I didn't want to exercise in the morning...

1498
01:01:10,777 --> 01:01:12,317
as my body felt so heavy.

1499
01:01:12,447 --> 01:01:14,386
I didn't really bother to exercise in the morning,

1500
01:01:14,387 --> 01:01:16,387
but running out at night all the time could get boring too.

1501
01:01:16,487 --> 01:01:19,526
I decided to join a running group in the mornings if I could find one.

1502
01:01:19,527 --> 01:01:21,727
I got used to it after attending a few times.

1503
01:01:22,057 --> 01:01:23,727
Gosh, it's 5am.

1504
01:01:23,897 --> 01:01:25,496
- Never. - You're a great runner.

1505
01:01:25,497 --> 01:01:26,826
- All right. - Hello.

1506
01:01:26,827 --> 01:01:29,196
- Hello. Good morning. - Hello.

1507
01:01:29,197 --> 01:01:30,567
- It's him? - Good morning.

1508
01:01:30,797 --> 01:01:32,067
It's the legend.

1509
01:01:32,737 --> 01:01:34,876
(Hip-hop duo Jinusean's main rapper, Sean)

1510
01:01:34,877 --> 01:01:35,977
It's been a while since I saw him.

1511
01:01:36,537 --> 01:01:39,277
He's originally a singer. He's a rapper.

1512
01:01:39,777 --> 01:01:42,576
What kind of image does Sean have in the hip-hop scene?

1513
01:01:42,577 --> 01:01:45,447
He's someone whom I find it hard to even mention.

1514
01:01:45,617 --> 01:01:47,886
- He's a great senior, right? - He's older than Gaeko.

1515
01:01:47,887 --> 01:01:49,287
- I become like this right away. - Right.

1516
01:01:49,417 --> 01:01:50,657
- Right. - Right.

1517
01:01:50,887 --> 01:01:53,987
He must be the one that loves running the most in our country.

1518
01:01:54,227 --> 01:01:56,257
He's the archaeopteryx.

1519
01:01:56,557 --> 01:01:57,797
(Actor Park Bo Gum)

1520
01:01:58,057 --> 01:01:59,267
(Actor Im Si Wan)

1521
01:01:59,667 --> 01:02:01,797
- This crew is really famous. - Right.

1522
01:02:02,267 --> 01:02:03,496
(Record of 7 hours, 50 minutes, and 22 seconds)

1523
01:02:03,521 --> 01:02:05,489
This really brought me to tears.

1524
01:02:07,467 --> 01:02:09,606
(The Seanshine of runners, Sean)

1525
01:02:09,607 --> 01:02:11,076
Professional athletes...

1526
01:02:11,077 --> 01:02:13,507
call it the mileage...

1527
01:02:13,907 --> 01:02:15,716
and they run 400km to 500km every month on average.

1528
01:02:15,717 --> 01:02:17,977
- Sean runs even more than that. - How much does he run?

1529
01:02:18,187 --> 01:02:20,287
He runs up to 700km a month.

1530
01:02:20,547 --> 01:02:22,516
He runs more than professional athletes?

1531
01:02:22,517 --> 01:02:24,117
He runs more than athletes.

1532
01:02:24,217 --> 01:02:26,686
He wasn't able to compete in competitions during COVID-19.

1533
01:02:26,687 --> 01:02:29,056
- At his terrace at home, - His terrace.

1534
01:02:29,057 --> 01:02:31,226
He ran back and forth in the space that is less than 10m...

1535
01:02:31,227 --> 01:02:33,227
- and completed 42.5km. - He completed that distance.

1536
01:02:33,697 --> 01:02:34,827
Gosh!

1537
01:02:35,867 --> 01:02:39,666
There were several times when we ran together at night.

1538
01:02:39,667 --> 01:02:42,177
He told me to come out for a run in the morning.

1539
01:02:42,477 --> 01:02:45,246
There's that feeling when you have to wake up at boot camp.

1540
01:02:45,247 --> 01:02:46,577
There's a day you really don't want to get up.

1541
01:02:46,747 --> 01:02:48,077
The blanket feels so warm.

1542
01:02:48,277 --> 01:02:49,976
You want to stay inside your cocoon.

1543
01:02:49,977 --> 01:02:52,646
If Sean was forceful...

1544
01:02:52,647 --> 01:02:55,256
or scary, I wouldn't have gone.

1545
01:02:55,257 --> 01:02:58,117
But there's that feeling you get from a very nice person.

1546
01:02:58,657 --> 01:03:01,057
"Let's run together." This sort of feeling.

1547
01:03:01,597 --> 01:03:05,066
You don't want to disappoint people like him.

1548
01:03:05,067 --> 01:03:06,727
- Right. - Some people are like that.

1549
01:03:07,227 --> 01:03:09,466
They all come out with the same thing in mind.

1550
01:03:09,467 --> 01:03:11,496
(They all come out with the same thing in mind.)

1551
01:03:11,497 --> 01:03:14,306
They have been caught by the trap of his smile.

1552
01:03:14,307 --> 01:03:15,906
- It's hard to refuse him. - Right.

1553
01:03:15,907 --> 01:03:16,977
I understand.

1554
01:03:17,277 --> 01:03:18,277
Hello, Sean.

1555
01:03:18,278 --> 01:03:19,907
- Was the run okay? - Yes.

1556
01:03:20,207 --> 01:03:22,276
- Shouldn't you get some rest? - Well...

1557
01:03:22,277 --> 01:03:23,517
It hasn't been long since the run.

1558
01:03:23,647 --> 01:03:25,147
I have to prepare for Sydney again.

1559
01:03:25,247 --> 01:03:26,316
You got some rest the day after...

1560
01:03:26,317 --> 01:03:27,317
National Liberation Day of Korea, right?

1561
01:03:27,417 --> 01:03:28,457
- No. - What?

1562
01:03:28,557 --> 01:03:30,186
You ran the next day right away?

1563
01:03:30,187 --> 01:03:31,687
- Yes. - Gosh!

1564
01:03:32,157 --> 01:03:34,427
I ran the day before yesterday and yesterday as well.

1565
01:03:35,257 --> 01:03:37,557
(The Unknown Crew members gather up one by one.)

1566
01:03:38,267 --> 01:03:39,826
(Peeking)

1567
01:03:39,827 --> 01:03:42,397
They were all great runners.

1568
01:03:42,597 --> 01:03:46,367
Young Pyo's legs were still about to burst open.

1569
01:03:46,637 --> 01:03:48,837
- He was a national team member. - Gosh. He was a great talent.

1570
01:03:48,937 --> 01:03:50,207
The icon of hard work.

1571
01:03:50,507 --> 01:03:52,277
(Soccer legend, Lee Young Pyo)

1572
01:03:52,977 --> 01:03:54,546
The gold medalist of wrestling,

1573
01:03:54,547 --> 01:03:55,847
- Jung Ji Hyun. - Jung Ji Hyun?

1574
01:03:56,647 --> 01:03:57,816
- Right. - He was a gold medalist...

1575
01:03:57,817 --> 01:03:59,086
- in wrestling. - Right.

1576
01:03:59,087 --> 01:04:00,686
(The Asian wrestling king that went beyond weight classes)

1577
01:04:00,687 --> 01:04:01,717
I know him.

1578
01:04:01,817 --> 01:04:04,417
Coach Kwon Eun Ju. Korea's best female marathoner.

1579
01:04:05,587 --> 01:04:06,726
- Gosh. - It's her?

1580
01:04:06,727 --> 01:04:08,057
I definitely know her.

1581
01:04:08,997 --> 01:04:10,056
She's an athlete?

1582
01:04:10,057 --> 01:04:11,057
(Marathoner, Lee Yeoun Jin)

1583
01:04:11,767 --> 01:04:12,767
(Track and field athlete, Jang Ho June)

1584
01:04:12,768 --> 01:04:14,767
- Why is an athlete there? - Actor Go Han Min.

1585
01:04:15,397 --> 01:04:16,836
I heard that Yoon Se A was a great runner too.

1586
01:04:16,837 --> 01:04:18,236
- They're all the ones... - Workout YouTuber,

1587
01:04:18,237 --> 01:04:19,906
- that are known for their running. - Shim Eu Ddeum.

1588
01:04:19,907 --> 01:04:21,466
They're all from the top tier. Gosh.

1589
01:04:21,467 --> 01:04:23,537
It was a very intense crew.

1590
01:04:24,037 --> 01:04:27,646
I always go with a heart of learning.

1591
01:04:27,647 --> 01:04:29,216
- Welcome. - Hello.

1592
01:04:29,217 --> 01:04:31,247
(Welcome.)

1593
01:04:31,687 --> 01:04:34,887
Let's warm up in a larger area.

1594
01:04:35,657 --> 01:04:37,816
We'll start our exercise.

1595
01:04:37,817 --> 01:04:39,757
Please form a circle.

1596
01:04:39,987 --> 01:04:42,227
Let's do our exercise. Let's roll our ankles.

1597
01:04:42,297 --> 01:04:43,897
One, two, three.

1598
01:04:44,357 --> 01:04:46,326
Five, six, seven, eight.

1599
01:04:46,327 --> 01:04:47,967
Two, two, three.

1600
01:04:48,267 --> 01:04:50,536
Five, six, seven, eight.

1601
01:04:50,537 --> 01:04:52,137
- One, two, three. - Four.

1602
01:04:52,367 --> 01:04:54,506
Five, six, seven, eight.

1603
01:04:54,507 --> 01:04:56,536
- Two, two, three, four. - Two, two, three, four.

1604
01:04:56,537 --> 01:04:58,876
- Five, six, seven, eight. - Five, six, seven, eight.

1605
01:04:58,877 --> 01:05:01,306
- Two, two, three, four. - Two, two, three, four.

1606
01:05:01,307 --> 01:05:03,847
- Five, six, seven, eight. - Why do you lack energy?

1607
01:05:04,577 --> 01:05:05,647
Three.

1608
01:05:05,817 --> 01:05:06,916
I couldn't get used to it because I don't run...

1609
01:05:06,917 --> 01:05:08,017
- in the morning. - One.

1610
01:05:08,587 --> 01:05:10,517
- How tiring. - My body wouldn't wake up.

1611
01:05:10,787 --> 01:05:12,586
That's all for the warmup.

1612
01:05:12,587 --> 01:05:14,156
We won't be able to run if we continue,

1613
01:05:14,157 --> 01:05:15,356
- so let's stop here. - Sounds good!

1614
01:05:15,357 --> 01:05:17,227
- Let's stop here. - Thank you.

1615
01:05:17,627 --> 01:05:18,726
All right.

1616
01:05:18,727 --> 01:05:19,866
Do we start right away?

1617
01:05:19,867 --> 01:05:21,267
Yes, we'll get moving now.

1618
01:05:21,897 --> 01:05:23,236
- We'll run 5km this way... - Yes.

1619
01:05:23,237 --> 01:05:24,596
- and come back to drink. - Okay.

1620
01:05:24,597 --> 01:05:26,837
Then we'll run 3km this way and come back.

1621
01:05:27,037 --> 01:05:28,777
If it's 15km, then...

1622
01:05:29,507 --> 01:05:31,106
It's 10km back and forth.

1623
01:05:31,107 --> 01:05:32,206
- It's 5km back and forth. - Oh, I see.

1624
01:05:32,207 --> 01:05:34,146
- It's 5km and 5km, so 10km. - Back and forth.

1625
01:05:34,147 --> 01:05:35,247
- Then it's 10km. - It's 10km.

1626
01:05:35,417 --> 01:05:37,677
It's 15km if we return from Seongsu Bridge.

1627
01:05:37,817 --> 01:05:39,246
That's 15km in total.

1628
01:05:39,247 --> 01:05:40,947
(The morning running course is 15km in total.)

1629
01:05:41,517 --> 01:05:42,856
Running 15km is long too.

1630
01:05:42,857 --> 01:05:43,986
- Running 15km is long too. - Of course it is.

1631
01:05:43,987 --> 01:05:45,126
It's in the morning.

1632
01:05:45,127 --> 01:05:46,587
- It doesn't end. - Running 15km is long too.

1633
01:05:47,687 --> 01:05:51,257
(He's run the full course before.)

1634
01:05:52,727 --> 01:05:54,267
Kian84 runs well.

1635
01:05:55,097 --> 01:05:57,167
Yes. New York City Marathon.

1636
01:05:58,967 --> 01:06:00,437
Let's get going.

1637
01:06:01,977 --> 01:06:03,206
Let's get moving.

1638
01:06:03,207 --> 01:06:04,237
(Nervous)

1639
01:06:04,637 --> 01:06:06,946
- It's really cool now. - It's gotten a lot cooler.

1640
01:06:06,947 --> 01:06:08,547
Right? It's cool now.

1641
01:06:08,777 --> 01:06:10,646
- Let's run in two lines. - Okay.

1642
01:06:10,647 --> 01:06:11,916
We'll start with a pace...

1643
01:06:11,917 --> 01:06:13,547
- of 6 minutes and 30 seconds. - Okay.

1644
01:06:15,087 --> 01:06:16,416
We got it today as well.

1645
01:06:16,417 --> 01:06:17,886
- Let's go. - Let's go.

1646
01:06:17,887 --> 01:06:19,056
Let's go.

1647
01:06:19,057 --> 01:06:20,427
(Unknown Crew and Kian84 start running!)

1648
01:06:20,987 --> 01:06:22,496
We got it today as well.

1649
01:06:22,497 --> 01:06:23,996
- Let's go. - Let's go.

1650
01:06:23,997 --> 01:06:25,126
Let's go.

1651
01:06:25,127 --> 01:06:26,367
(They set off while cheering for themselves.)

1652
01:06:28,027 --> 01:06:29,536
(Clearing his throat)

1653
01:06:29,537 --> 01:06:30,637
Show them.

1654
01:06:31,237 --> 01:06:32,367
Show them what you got.

1655
01:06:33,507 --> 01:06:34,507
At the pace...

1656
01:06:34,508 --> 01:06:35,906
- of 6 minutes and 30 seconds... - We'll run 2km.

1657
01:06:35,907 --> 01:06:37,336
- And then? - It'll be six minutes.

1658
01:06:37,337 --> 01:06:38,447
Six minutes.

1659
01:06:39,377 --> 01:06:40,746
I started running recently too,

1660
01:06:40,747 --> 01:06:43,646
and running at the pace of 6 minutes and 30 seconds...

1661
01:06:43,647 --> 01:06:44,816
is not that slow.

1662
01:06:44,817 --> 01:06:45,817
(They're to maintain the pace of 6 minutes per kilometer.)

1663
01:06:46,487 --> 01:06:48,586
Look at the sky.

1664
01:06:48,587 --> 01:06:50,057
Gosh, look to your left!

1665
01:06:51,057 --> 01:06:52,156
- It's pretty. - It's pretty.

1666
01:06:52,157 --> 01:06:53,357
Gosh, the sun is coming up.

1667
01:06:53,487 --> 01:06:55,156
- It's pretty. - That was the nice part.

1668
01:06:55,157 --> 01:06:56,257
Seeing the sun rise.

1669
01:06:57,427 --> 01:06:59,996
Time goes really fast, and so does the distance.

1670
01:06:59,997 --> 01:07:01,327
I think so too.

1671
01:07:01,867 --> 01:07:03,797
- If you run alone... - I can't run alone anymore.

1672
01:07:04,337 --> 01:07:06,167
I've gotten a disease of not being able to run alone.

1673
01:07:08,437 --> 01:07:10,806
- It's too fast! - Stop chattering!

1674
01:07:10,807 --> 01:07:11,907
Okay.

1675
01:07:12,847 --> 01:07:14,477
- Is it raining? - It's raining.

1676
01:07:14,707 --> 01:07:16,076
- It's raining. - It's so nice.

1677
01:07:16,077 --> 01:07:17,677
- Right? - I hope it pours.

1678
01:07:17,817 --> 01:07:18,887
It's raining?

1679
01:07:19,487 --> 01:07:21,917
It was really humid and hot.

1680
01:07:23,757 --> 01:07:26,827
I hate it when it rains because it gets icky.

1681
01:07:27,057 --> 01:07:29,057
But when I run, it's the best if it rains.

1682
01:07:29,497 --> 01:07:32,067
I think it's even better when the rain is heavy.

1683
01:07:32,527 --> 01:07:34,096
When I ran with the Yeouido crew,

1684
01:07:34,097 --> 01:07:35,667
- it rained heavily too. - That's right.

1685
01:07:35,937 --> 01:07:37,696
If you run in the rain,

1686
01:07:37,697 --> 01:07:40,167
you become a bit crazy.

1687
01:07:40,437 --> 01:07:41,906
You go a little crazy.

1688
01:07:41,907 --> 01:07:45,277
So I hoped it'd rain more.

1689
01:07:46,007 --> 01:07:47,847
(The rain pours down refreshingly.)

1690
01:07:48,207 --> 01:07:49,846
Doesn't it get slippery?

1691
01:07:49,847 --> 01:07:51,117
- It doesn't. - It doesn't?

1692
01:07:51,547 --> 01:07:52,647
- It's perfect when you run. - Let's go.

1693
01:07:54,147 --> 01:07:55,716
(The crew members are happy even when they get rained on.)

1694
01:07:55,717 --> 01:07:57,416
They look so happy.

1695
01:07:57,417 --> 01:07:58,727
They all look so bright.

1696
01:07:59,027 --> 01:08:01,427
(Gosh, it's refreshing.)

1697
01:08:02,127 --> 01:08:03,627
It's raining!

1698
01:08:05,897 --> 01:08:06,897
I didn't know,

1699
01:08:06,897 --> 01:08:07,897
- but he likes being in the rain. - I love running in the rain.

1700
01:08:07,898 --> 01:08:09,167
One, two, three!

1701
01:08:09,437 --> 01:08:10,967
It's raining!

1702
01:08:11,437 --> 01:08:12,836
Oh, my.

1703
01:08:12,837 --> 01:08:13,967
(Madness)

1704
01:08:15,777 --> 01:08:17,936
All right. We've run 2km so far.

1705
01:08:17,937 --> 01:08:19,647
(They finish running the 2km section in the rain.)

1706
01:08:20,147 --> 01:08:21,247
Go straight.

1707
01:08:22,117 --> 01:08:23,177
Go straight.

1708
01:08:24,617 --> 01:08:26,517
We'll run straight and go there.

1709
01:08:27,317 --> 01:08:28,887
There are fewer people, aren't there?

1710
01:08:29,017 --> 01:08:31,816
The group that runs slower...

1711
01:08:31,817 --> 01:08:33,227
runs separately in the back.

1712
01:08:34,627 --> 01:08:36,556
(They've run until Ichon-dong for 4km now.)

1713
01:08:36,557 --> 01:08:40,097
(Ichon, Oksu Station)

1714
01:08:41,167 --> 01:08:42,696
(It's 5:40am.)

1715
01:08:42,697 --> 01:08:44,237
(Running in the morning is a little hard.)

1716
01:08:45,507 --> 01:08:47,307
The halfway point is up ahead.

1717
01:08:48,807 --> 01:08:51,507
We'll turn right here and make a big round to the left.

1718
01:08:52,007 --> 01:08:53,707
- From here. - Halfway.

1719
01:08:53,847 --> 01:08:55,046
Turn. Okay.

1720
01:08:55,047 --> 01:08:56,946
One. Let's turn.

1721
01:08:56,947 --> 01:08:58,987
- Where is he going? - Where are you going, Kian84?

1722
01:09:01,147 --> 01:09:02,256
He has no sense of direction.

1723
01:09:02,257 --> 01:09:03,657
(He turned the opposite direction by himself.)

1724
01:09:04,857 --> 01:09:05,887
Let's go!

1725
01:09:06,027 --> 01:09:07,056
- Let's go. - Let's go.

1726
01:09:07,057 --> 01:09:08,186
Oh, hello!

1727
01:09:08,187 --> 01:09:09,927
- Hello. - Hello.

1728
01:09:10,657 --> 01:09:13,726
- Let's go, everyone! - If you run at your pace,

1729
01:09:13,727 --> 01:09:15,337
- Where did she go? - it puts you in a good mood.

1730
01:09:15,737 --> 01:09:17,337
They run so happily.

1731
01:09:19,507 --> 01:09:20,607
This is nice.

1732
01:09:21,037 --> 01:09:22,337
- Isn't it amazing? - It is.

1733
01:09:22,877 --> 01:09:23,936
It's beautiful.

1734
01:09:23,937 --> 01:09:25,136
Gosh, look at the back.

1735
01:09:25,137 --> 01:09:28,407
That was the nice part about running in the morning.

1736
01:09:28,717 --> 01:09:29,776
It's beautiful.

1737
01:09:29,777 --> 01:09:31,876
(The sky has completely brightened.)

1738
01:09:31,877 --> 01:09:33,286
You get to see the sun rise.

1739
01:09:33,287 --> 01:09:34,346
Let's go.

1740
01:09:34,347 --> 01:09:36,117
I loved the smell of early morning.

1741
01:09:37,957 --> 01:09:42,457
(He welcomes the morning with the sunrise while running.)

1742
01:09:43,327 --> 01:09:45,226
(They run straight while taking in the refreshing air.)

1743
01:09:45,227 --> 01:09:46,267
Two lines.

1744
01:09:46,597 --> 01:09:47,997
This is Jamsu Bridge.

1745
01:09:49,997 --> 01:09:51,636
(There's an uphill road ahead.)

1746
01:09:51,637 --> 01:09:52,737
Gosh.

1747
01:09:53,437 --> 01:09:54,966
- When you run uphill, - Bicycles, let's go!

1748
01:09:54,967 --> 01:09:56,036
- you slow down. - Yes.

1749
01:09:56,037 --> 01:09:57,306
(Slow down when running uphill.)

1750
01:09:57,307 --> 01:09:59,307
- With shorter strides. - Yes, run with shorter strides.

1751
01:09:59,677 --> 01:10:02,017
Don't look far ahead. Just look about 3m ahead.

1752
01:10:05,277 --> 01:10:07,347
(It gets faster downhill.)

1753
01:10:08,317 --> 01:10:09,757
How do you feel today?

1754
01:10:10,317 --> 01:10:11,387
I'm tired.

1755
01:10:12,457 --> 01:10:14,887
It's because you aren't fully recovered yet.

1756
01:10:15,557 --> 01:10:18,027
(Sean looks bright even when he runs while talking.)

1757
01:10:18,697 --> 01:10:21,597
(On the other hand, Kian84's smile completely disappeared.)

1758
01:10:23,237 --> 01:10:24,936
(As he runs at the pace of the crew members who run and smile...)

1759
01:10:24,937 --> 01:10:26,367
- Let's go. - Let's go.

1760
01:10:28,607 --> 01:10:29,876
- We'll turn around up there. - Turn around.

1761
01:10:29,877 --> 01:10:30,976
They made it halfway.

1762
01:10:30,977 --> 01:10:32,077
Be careful.

1763
01:10:32,507 --> 01:10:33,676
(Ichon, Oksu Station)

1764
01:10:33,677 --> 01:10:34,806
Turn.

1765
01:10:34,807 --> 01:10:36,276
(Southern part of Jamsu Bridge)

1766
01:10:36,277 --> 01:10:37,417
All right.

1767
01:10:38,077 --> 01:10:40,647
(They don't slow down even after running half the course.)

1768
01:10:41,347 --> 01:10:43,687
(Then they face an uphill road again.)

1769
01:10:45,287 --> 01:10:47,857
(Gosh, I can't make it this way.)

1770
01:10:49,757 --> 01:10:50,896
Sean, isn't it too fast?

1771
01:10:50,897 --> 01:10:52,467
(Laughing)

1772
01:10:52,867 --> 01:10:54,027
Let's go!

1773
01:10:54,397 --> 01:10:57,097
The pacemaker should keep the pace well.

1774
01:10:57,497 --> 01:11:00,107
But Sean wasn't so good at keeping the pace.

1775
01:11:00,637 --> 01:11:01,907
- He's too fast, right? - Yes.

1776
01:11:02,207 --> 01:11:04,576
He keeps running fast if I don't stop him.

1777
01:11:04,577 --> 01:11:05,946
Doesn't he check the pace...

1778
01:11:05,947 --> 01:11:07,877
- while running? - He rarely did.

1779
01:11:08,177 --> 01:11:10,247
(Sean only looked forward and ran.)

1780
01:11:12,787 --> 01:11:13,946
Sean, it's 5 minutes per 1km.

1781
01:11:13,947 --> 01:11:15,016
- Gosh, sorry. - We've made it...

1782
01:11:15,017 --> 01:11:16,686
to 4 minutes and 55 seconds.

1783
01:11:16,687 --> 01:11:18,687
Sean keeps running faster.

1784
01:11:18,957 --> 01:11:21,357
He ran up until four-minute range when I didn't stop him.

1785
01:11:22,697 --> 01:11:24,527
Maybe I wasn't used to it.

1786
01:11:24,857 --> 01:11:28,467
Or maybe I was a little intimidated to run with good runners.

1787
01:11:28,937 --> 01:11:30,767
It was really tiring.

1788
01:11:31,097 --> 01:11:33,307
You run for the fun of giving pointers.

1789
01:11:33,707 --> 01:11:36,407
But when you're with people who run way better than you,

1790
01:11:36,977 --> 01:11:38,107
you get intimidated.

1791
01:11:38,577 --> 01:11:41,606
I thought I might be a burden to them.

1792
01:11:41,607 --> 01:11:42,877
I couldn't help but be self-conscious.

1793
01:11:43,147 --> 01:11:44,347
The pace is good.

1794
01:11:45,547 --> 01:11:47,917
(This is a good pace?)

1795
01:11:48,787 --> 01:11:50,257
I don't think you should be a pacemaker.

1796
01:11:51,357 --> 01:11:54,456
The coach should be a pacemaker.

1797
01:11:54,457 --> 01:11:55,557
(You're running too fast.)

1798
01:11:57,097 --> 01:11:58,326
I'm sorry. It's 5 minutes and 40 seconds.

1799
01:11:58,327 --> 01:11:59,367
It's 5 minutes and 40 seconds.

1800
01:11:59,827 --> 01:12:01,567
I'll keep it at six minutes.

1801
01:12:03,167 --> 01:12:04,336
Sean, it's 5 minutes and 45 seconds.

1802
01:12:04,337 --> 01:12:05,466
How come he's so fast?

1803
01:12:05,467 --> 01:12:07,437
- We need to go 15 seconds slower. - Okay.

1804
01:12:08,707 --> 01:12:09,777
- Sean. - Yes?

1805
01:12:10,077 --> 01:12:11,406
It's 5 minutes and 30 seconds now.

1806
01:12:11,407 --> 01:12:13,277
- Oh, okay. - All right.

1807
01:12:13,777 --> 01:12:15,147
Actually, it was 5 minutes and 25 seconds.

1808
01:12:16,517 --> 01:12:19,387
(As they ran with all their might, )

1809
01:12:19,917 --> 01:12:21,316
(they ran past the 10km point.)

1810
01:12:21,317 --> 01:12:22,986
- They run so well. - Gosh.

1811
01:12:22,987 --> 01:12:24,086
That's a lot.

1812
01:12:24,087 --> 01:12:25,087
(They ran past the 10km point.)

1813
01:12:26,587 --> 01:12:28,127
(Glancing over)

1814
01:12:29,727 --> 01:12:31,426
What's your heart rate now?

1815
01:12:31,427 --> 01:12:33,367
- It's 125bpm. - It's really low.

1816
01:12:33,767 --> 01:12:34,866
People who run well...

1817
01:12:34,867 --> 01:12:35,997
- have a low heart rate. - Of course.

1818
01:12:37,097 --> 01:12:38,637
It's pretty low considering the pace.

1819
01:12:39,367 --> 01:12:41,037
Mine is 140bpm.

1820
01:12:41,407 --> 01:12:43,376
- It's not bad if it's 140bpm. - Is it?

1821
01:12:43,377 --> 01:12:45,177
Ours would shoot up to 160bpm.

1822
01:12:46,347 --> 01:12:47,977
It changes depending on the weather,

1823
01:12:48,917 --> 01:12:50,777
and it depends on the time of the day too.

1824
01:12:52,887 --> 01:12:57,417
(Regardless of my heart rate, let's just look ahead and run.)

1825
01:12:59,627 --> 01:13:00,887
The view is amazing.

1826
01:13:05,497 --> 01:13:06,897
Gosh, it's amazing.

1827
01:13:08,027 --> 01:13:10,937
(The running crew burn up with the sun.)

1828
01:13:11,637 --> 01:13:12,807
It must be really tiring.

1829
01:13:13,307 --> 01:13:14,607
He runs so well, though.

1830
01:13:15,267 --> 01:13:18,277
(They've made past the 13km point.)

1831
01:13:19,277 --> 01:13:23,476
(Even if he falls behind for a second, )

1832
01:13:23,477 --> 01:13:25,477
(he keeps going forward with perseverance.)

1833
01:13:25,717 --> 01:13:28,787
Kian84. All right. Let's go.

1834
01:13:29,557 --> 01:13:31,217
We have 500m to go.

1835
01:13:31,917 --> 01:13:34,287
(Let's hang in there a little longer.)

1836
01:13:34,587 --> 01:13:37,596
Okay, we'll sprint after 50m.

1837
01:13:37,597 --> 01:13:38,627
(Let's go!)

1838
01:13:39,627 --> 01:13:41,567
Thirty, twenty.

1839
01:13:44,537 --> 01:13:46,167
Ten. Get ready.

1840
01:13:46,737 --> 01:13:47,737
Go.

1841
01:13:47,738 --> 01:13:49,376
We're ending it with the last interval run.

1842
01:13:49,377 --> 01:13:50,507
Interval?

1843
01:13:50,637 --> 01:13:52,676
- It kills you. The last one. - A sprint?

1844
01:13:52,677 --> 01:13:55,107
(Gathering every ounce of energy they have)

1845
01:13:55,507 --> 01:13:56,976
This really kills you.

1846
01:13:56,977 --> 01:13:58,916
(Running at full speed)

1847
01:13:58,917 --> 01:14:01,016
We raised our heart rate to the fullest...

1848
01:14:01,017 --> 01:14:02,516
- before ending the run. - That's right.

1849
01:14:02,517 --> 01:14:04,157
(Maximizing cardiovascular endurance with maximum heart rate)

1850
01:14:05,287 --> 01:14:07,357
- All right. - They run so well.

1851
01:14:08,527 --> 01:14:10,397
Slow down. Don't stop.

1852
01:14:10,497 --> 01:14:11,796
They ran that much that early in the morning?

1853
01:14:11,797 --> 01:14:13,096
It's unbelievable.

1854
01:14:13,097 --> 01:14:15,796
Because it was in the morning,

1855
01:14:15,797 --> 01:14:18,136
we ran slowly.

1856
01:14:18,137 --> 01:14:20,366
You can only burn 852 calories after running that much?

1857
01:14:20,367 --> 01:14:22,106
- Gosh. - It's so not time-effective, right?

1858
01:14:22,107 --> 01:14:23,176
- It's usually... - It's not even enough...

1859
01:14:23,177 --> 01:14:24,236
for a bowl of jjajangmyeon.

1860
01:14:24,237 --> 01:14:26,306
But you know it's the most effective exercise, right?

1861
01:14:26,307 --> 01:14:28,006
- Running is most effective. - Other workouts...

1862
01:14:28,007 --> 01:14:29,317
- That's true. - Really?

1863
01:14:29,747 --> 01:14:31,747
- Good work. - Great job.

1864
01:14:32,217 --> 01:14:33,617
You run so well.

1865
01:14:34,187 --> 01:14:35,446
You go separate ways after this, right?

1866
01:14:35,447 --> 01:14:37,287
Yes, we get dismissed after running.

1867
01:14:37,687 --> 01:14:38,927
Great job, director.

1868
01:14:39,457 --> 01:14:40,526
Let's go.

1869
01:14:40,527 --> 01:14:41,786
- Shall we run more? - Sorry?

1870
01:14:41,787 --> 01:14:42,897
- "Shall we run more?" - Let's go.

1871
01:14:43,657 --> 01:14:44,756
Well done, everyone.

1872
01:14:44,757 --> 01:14:47,297
(Among the lively veterans...)

1873
01:14:50,337 --> 01:14:51,337
(I'm dying.)

1874
01:14:51,338 --> 01:14:53,367
My goodness.

1875
01:14:58,947 --> 01:15:00,106
Thank you.

1876
01:15:00,107 --> 01:15:01,807
(The heartwarming crew members take care of the newbie.)

1877
01:15:03,277 --> 01:15:04,376
Great job.

1878
01:15:04,377 --> 01:15:05,447
Great job.

1879
01:15:06,117 --> 01:15:08,746
I don't think I'll be able to go on with the day after this.

1880
01:15:08,747 --> 01:15:09,817
Me neither.

1881
01:15:10,857 --> 01:15:12,527
Gosh, running in the morning is tough.

1882
01:15:14,087 --> 01:15:15,486
So if you...

1883
01:15:15,487 --> 01:15:17,996
come out and run in the morning 2 or 3 times a week,

1884
01:15:17,997 --> 01:15:19,896
you'll get a lot more comfortable.

1885
01:15:19,897 --> 01:15:21,667
He's talking with a fairy-like smile.

1886
01:15:22,297 --> 01:15:23,997
- I'll come out a few more times. - Yes, keep coming.

1887
01:15:25,197 --> 01:15:27,237
It's... What time is it?

1888
01:15:28,107 --> 01:15:29,737
So your day starts now?

1889
01:15:29,937 --> 01:15:30,937
What do you do?

1890
01:15:30,938 --> 01:15:32,746
I take my kids to school,

1891
01:15:32,747 --> 01:15:34,747
then I get ready. For today,

1892
01:15:34,977 --> 01:15:36,317
there's an MOU signing...

1893
01:15:36,717 --> 01:15:39,047
for Seungil Hope Foundation, so I'm going there.

1894
01:15:39,247 --> 01:15:41,816
I have a YouTube shoot at 1pm.

1895
01:15:41,817 --> 01:15:44,557
After that, I'm going to volunteer work...

1896
01:15:44,717 --> 01:15:46,357
at a child rehab center at 7pm.

1897
01:15:46,957 --> 01:15:49,357
- Donation fairy. - I'm packed until the evening.

1898
01:15:49,827 --> 01:15:51,996
- How can one live like that? - I know.

1899
01:15:51,997 --> 01:15:54,097
- He's amazing. - He's incredible.

1900
01:15:54,897 --> 01:15:56,097
Don't you take a nap?

1901
01:15:56,397 --> 01:15:57,667
I don't.

1902
01:15:58,137 --> 01:15:59,707
He only sleeps four hours a day.

1903
01:16:00,337 --> 01:16:01,407
Goodness.

1904
01:16:02,037 --> 01:16:04,107
(Hesitating)

1905
01:16:05,077 --> 01:16:06,547
Are you happy?

1906
01:16:08,847 --> 01:16:10,416
It's a sincere question.

1907
01:16:10,417 --> 01:16:11,817
That's the question I wanted to ask.

1908
01:16:12,077 --> 01:16:13,446
Everyone must've been curious.

1909
01:16:13,447 --> 01:16:15,686
I was a little out of it. That's why I asked him that.

1910
01:16:15,687 --> 01:16:16,817
Well done.

1911
01:16:17,317 --> 01:16:19,786
- Aren't you fooling yourself? - No.

1912
01:16:19,787 --> 01:16:21,127
"I'm happy."

1913
01:16:21,627 --> 01:16:23,157
I still have this habit.

1914
01:16:23,297 --> 01:16:26,127
I'd criticize a lot as a webtoonist.

1915
01:16:26,427 --> 01:16:27,797
"He must be..."

1916
01:16:27,897 --> 01:16:30,467
"thinking otherwise deep inside."

1917
01:16:30,937 --> 01:16:33,107
I was curious about him...

1918
01:16:33,237 --> 01:16:35,067
because he does so many good deeds.

1919
01:16:35,407 --> 01:16:37,406
He's like a hero...

1920
01:16:37,407 --> 01:16:39,606
who'd appear in textbooks.

1921
01:16:39,607 --> 01:16:42,107
- I know. - How can one live like that?

1922
01:16:42,377 --> 01:16:44,946
If you eat only organic food every day,

1923
01:16:44,947 --> 01:16:46,586
- you want to have instant food too. - That's right.

1924
01:16:46,587 --> 01:16:48,417
- Wouldn't he want that too? - Since he's a human too.

1925
01:16:49,287 --> 01:16:51,816
- Aren't you fooling yourself? - No.

1926
01:16:51,817 --> 01:16:53,257
"I'm happy."

1927
01:16:53,527 --> 01:16:55,126
- I'm really happy. - "I'm having fun."

1928
01:16:55,127 --> 01:16:57,057
- If I were really fooling myself, - Yes.

1929
01:16:57,157 --> 01:16:58,556
There'd be a point...

1930
01:16:58,557 --> 01:17:00,867
- where I give up. - That's true.

1931
01:17:01,827 --> 01:17:03,237
He's consistent too.

1932
01:17:05,637 --> 01:17:07,107
- How about 5km more? - Let's go.

1933
01:17:07,467 --> 01:17:09,177
- For real? - Gosh.

1934
01:17:10,107 --> 01:17:11,237
Go ahead.

1935
01:17:12,077 --> 01:17:13,546
- See you again. - Have fun.

1936
01:17:13,547 --> 01:17:14,746
- Yes. - Let's go!

1937
01:17:14,747 --> 01:17:15,776
- Let's go. - Let's go.

1938
01:17:15,777 --> 01:17:16,877
Gosh, they're amazing.

1939
01:17:17,247 --> 01:17:19,587
- He's out of energy. - Gosh, it's hot.

1940
01:17:20,487 --> 01:17:22,016
Let's stretch.

1941
01:17:22,017 --> 01:17:23,487
- Okay. - Okay.

1942
01:17:23,657 --> 01:17:25,756
- Let's start stretching. - All right.

1943
01:17:25,757 --> 01:17:27,986
Grab your head and pull down.

1944
01:17:27,987 --> 01:17:30,556
- One, two. - Oh, the post-run stretch.

1945
01:17:30,557 --> 01:17:32,826
- Three. One, two... - It's a healthy way to start a day.

1946
01:17:32,827 --> 01:17:33,897
And seven.

1947
01:17:35,067 --> 01:17:36,836
Seven, eight.

1948
01:17:36,837 --> 01:17:38,396
We'll wrap up here.

1949
01:17:38,397 --> 01:17:39,866
- Great job. - At 4am...

1950
01:17:39,867 --> 01:17:41,367
- Good job. - They're amazing.

1951
01:17:41,507 --> 01:17:43,736
- Great job. - Great job.

1952
01:17:43,737 --> 01:17:45,807
- Look at him. He's exhausted. - Great job.

1953
01:17:46,007 --> 01:17:47,206
Great job.

1954
01:17:47,207 --> 01:17:48,647
(Screaming)

1955
01:17:48,747 --> 01:17:50,617
The new member of Unknown Crew.

1956
01:17:50,677 --> 01:17:52,316
- What? - A new member?

1957
01:17:52,317 --> 01:17:53,817
- He's done for. - Is he an official member now?

1958
01:17:54,217 --> 01:17:55,487
The new member of Unknown Crew.

1959
01:17:55,747 --> 01:17:57,556
- I'll come back next year. - Right?

1960
01:17:57,557 --> 01:17:59,357
- I have something to give you. - What?

1961
01:17:59,527 --> 01:18:02,726
We got you headphones and a T-shirt.

1962
01:18:02,727 --> 01:18:04,326
Can I take them next time?

1963
01:18:04,327 --> 01:18:06,326
He's trying to leave as soon as he can.

1964
01:18:06,327 --> 01:18:07,496
He's hurrying off.

1965
01:18:07,497 --> 01:18:09,497
- I'll come back for the interview. - All right.

1966
01:18:09,537 --> 01:18:10,766
- Great job. - All right.

1967
01:18:10,767 --> 01:18:12,196
- Bye. - Good job.

1968
01:18:12,197 --> 01:18:13,237
Great job.

1969
01:18:15,137 --> 01:18:17,037
(End of running, 8:30am)

1970
01:18:19,877 --> 01:18:21,747
- He must be tired. - He must be tired.

1971
01:18:22,047 --> 01:18:24,116
I think I'll have to...

1972
01:18:24,117 --> 01:18:25,986
take a nap for three hours in that state.

1973
01:18:25,987 --> 01:18:27,017
I know.

1974
01:18:27,347 --> 01:18:30,357
(Kian84 trudges somewhere.)

1975
01:18:30,417 --> 01:18:31,557
Gosh, it's hot.

1976
01:18:32,727 --> 01:18:34,226
(Jamwon Swimming Pool)

1977
01:18:34,227 --> 01:18:35,956
- What? - A swimming pool?

1978
01:18:35,957 --> 01:18:38,057
- It's popular these days. - It is.

1979
01:18:39,427 --> 01:18:40,826
Thank you.

1980
01:18:40,827 --> 01:18:42,566
- Hello. - Hello.

1981
01:18:42,567 --> 01:18:45,437
- One adult, please. - Okay. Please insert your card.

1982
01:18:45,497 --> 01:18:48,006
I saw this place...

1983
01:18:48,007 --> 01:18:49,376
every time I ran in the evening.

1984
01:18:49,377 --> 01:18:52,876
It's steaming hot after a run during summer.

1985
01:18:52,877 --> 01:18:55,707
I've always wanted to go into the pool.

1986
01:18:55,777 --> 01:18:57,846
Since I was soaked in sweat anyway,

1987
01:18:57,847 --> 01:19:00,217
I thought I might as well just go.

1988
01:19:01,587 --> 01:19:03,787
- It's pretty. - Gosh.

1989
01:19:03,987 --> 01:19:05,956
It's really colorful.

1990
01:19:05,957 --> 01:19:06,987
It's nice, right?

1991
01:19:08,757 --> 01:19:10,656
There weren't that many people that day.

1992
01:19:10,657 --> 01:19:11,726
Because it was in the morning.

1993
01:19:11,727 --> 01:19:13,597
- Oh, right. It's in the morning. - That's right.

1994
01:19:13,767 --> 01:19:15,767
My shirt stinks because I sweated.

1995
01:19:18,637 --> 01:19:21,207
(I should wash up first.)

1996
01:19:22,037 --> 01:19:24,537
(Taking off the socks that were kept in the shoes for 15km)

1997
01:19:26,047 --> 01:19:28,177
(Getting up)

1998
01:19:30,247 --> 01:19:33,247
- Gosh. - Gosh, that must be refreshing.

1999
01:19:33,447 --> 01:19:36,216
- Was it cold water? - Water gushes out there.

2000
01:19:36,217 --> 01:19:37,257
Goodness.

2001
01:19:41,757 --> 01:19:43,226
- Gosh! - Goodness.

2002
01:19:43,227 --> 01:19:44,497
Gosh, the crack wash.

2003
01:19:47,997 --> 01:19:49,997
(Feeling liberated from the run)

2004
01:19:52,537 --> 01:19:53,906
(Time to scrub away dirt from the morning run)

2005
01:19:53,907 --> 01:19:56,277
- Oh, is it soap? - That's body wash.

2006
01:19:58,737 --> 01:20:00,347
It looks really refreshing.

2007
01:20:02,077 --> 01:20:04,416
Running makes you happier.

2008
01:20:04,417 --> 01:20:07,716
Running for an hour and a half was torturing, to be honest.

2009
01:20:07,717 --> 01:20:09,047
But after that,

2010
01:20:09,457 --> 01:20:12,787
drinks taste about eight times better.

2011
01:20:13,157 --> 01:20:15,727
I feel about four times happier when I shower.

2012
01:20:16,827 --> 01:20:17,927
Gosh, how refreshing.

2013
01:20:19,627 --> 01:20:22,066
(He takes his clothes...)

2014
01:20:22,067 --> 01:20:23,567
(and goes to the changing room.)

2015
01:20:24,897 --> 01:20:26,267
(He strides forward in his swimsuit.)

2016
01:20:26,737 --> 01:20:28,006
I should have ramyeon.

2017
01:20:28,007 --> 01:20:29,337
They sell ramyeon too?

2018
01:20:32,237 --> 01:20:33,506
(Han River ramyeon and sundae)

2019
01:20:33,507 --> 01:20:35,576
- Oh, my. That's nice. - That's nice.

2020
01:20:35,577 --> 01:20:38,276
- I sweated too much, - Again?

2021
01:20:38,277 --> 01:20:40,216
- so I craved it so much. - It looks so good.

2022
01:20:40,217 --> 01:20:41,887
Han River ramyeon is delicious.

2023
01:20:43,417 --> 01:20:44,556
(Happy)

2024
01:20:44,557 --> 01:20:45,616
I want to eat ramyeon.

2025
01:20:45,617 --> 01:20:47,757
- It looks amazing. - Gosh, seriously.

2026
01:20:48,487 --> 01:20:49,556
(Taking a bite of sundae while waiting)

2027
01:20:49,557 --> 01:20:52,767
- That's what you call happiness. - It must be good.

2028
01:20:57,797 --> 01:20:59,706
(Taking a sip of the pickled radish juice)

2029
01:20:59,707 --> 01:21:01,007
It must be sour and nice.

2030
01:21:01,207 --> 01:21:03,006
Gosh, he drinks it like it's soda.

2031
01:21:03,007 --> 01:21:04,106
So he drinks that too?

2032
01:21:04,107 --> 01:21:05,107
(Pouring)

2033
01:21:05,108 --> 01:21:06,376
(What is that?)

2034
01:21:06,377 --> 01:21:08,307
- What is that? - It's sour and nice.

2035
01:21:08,407 --> 01:21:11,446
It's like tom yum kung.

2036
01:21:11,447 --> 01:21:12,647
- Sour... - It's sour and salty.

2037
01:21:14,917 --> 01:21:16,957
(He's done making tom yum kung ramyeon.)

2038
01:21:17,917 --> 01:21:20,057
(Shall I have the first meal of the day?)

2039
01:21:21,887 --> 01:21:23,426
He's the only one eating.

2040
01:21:23,427 --> 01:21:24,696
That's the nice thing about mornings.

2041
01:21:24,697 --> 01:21:26,597
It'd have been too crowded at night.

2042
01:21:28,427 --> 01:21:30,067
(First meal of the day, 9:30am)

2043
01:21:34,807 --> 01:21:36,237
Take a big bite.

2044
01:21:37,837 --> 01:21:40,147
- Gosh, that must be good. - Goodness.

2045
01:21:43,777 --> 01:21:44,777
It's amazing.

2046
01:21:45,947 --> 01:21:47,487
(This time, he has a bite with pickled radish.)

2047
01:21:54,157 --> 01:21:56,127
Because I sweated a lot,

2048
01:21:56,427 --> 01:21:57,856
I craved salt so much.

2049
01:21:57,857 --> 01:22:00,197
It was almost like...

2050
01:22:00,467 --> 01:22:03,297
my cells absorbing salt.

2051
01:22:04,067 --> 01:22:05,537
It was like my skin absorbing salt.

2052
01:22:07,837 --> 01:22:10,537
They sucked in the salt by themselves.

2053
01:22:15,277 --> 01:22:16,946
They absorbed it straight.

2054
01:22:16,947 --> 01:22:18,517
(Absorbing salt right away)

2055
01:22:20,117 --> 01:22:21,616
It almost drove me crazy.

2056
01:22:21,617 --> 01:22:22,787
(Salt hits his body fast.)

2057
01:22:24,087 --> 01:22:26,157
Gosh, how come I never get sick of ramyeon?

2058
01:22:29,597 --> 01:22:33,097
(That was a satisfying first meal.)

2059
01:22:37,097 --> 01:22:39,337
You punk. Why are you in there?

2060
01:22:45,837 --> 01:22:47,947
(I'm full.)

2061
01:22:48,307 --> 01:22:51,077
We'll open the pool now.

2062
01:22:52,077 --> 01:22:53,547
(He's ready with goggles.)

2063
01:22:57,717 --> 01:23:00,427
Gosh, people practice swimming there.

2064
01:23:00,557 --> 01:23:02,027
It's quite deep.

2065
01:23:03,427 --> 01:23:04,797
- Hello. - Hello.

2066
01:23:06,567 --> 01:23:09,436
Is it divided into different levels?

2067
01:23:09,437 --> 01:23:11,137
- I don't think so. - Oh, really?

2068
01:23:11,697 --> 01:23:13,667
Can you swim to the other end?

2069
01:23:14,807 --> 01:23:15,867
Is it 25m?

2070
01:23:16,107 --> 01:23:17,506
- I think it's 50m. - It's 50m?

2071
01:23:17,507 --> 01:23:19,376
- Yes. - It's not easy to swim 50m.

2072
01:23:19,377 --> 01:23:20,947
- It was quite long. - Of course.

2073
01:23:22,577 --> 01:23:24,676
- Swim to the other end? - They're quite good.

2074
01:23:24,677 --> 01:23:26,146
Just like running every day,

2075
01:23:26,147 --> 01:23:28,417
there are people who come to swim every day.

2076
01:23:28,547 --> 01:23:30,457
They can swim all they want outdoors.

2077
01:23:32,017 --> 01:23:33,827
(It's finally his turn.)

2078
01:23:35,127 --> 01:23:37,797
(He relaxedly sets off in freestyle.)

2079
01:23:45,397 --> 01:23:46,466
- Where is he going? - What?

2080
01:23:46,467 --> 01:23:47,606
- Gosh. - He's going over the lane.

2081
01:23:47,607 --> 01:23:49,336
- What is he doing? - Oh, gosh.

2082
01:23:49,337 --> 01:23:51,337
He might bump into them. No.

2083
01:23:52,237 --> 01:23:53,446
Why is he swimming like that?

2084
01:23:53,447 --> 01:23:55,177
(He swims forward diagonally.)

2085
01:23:57,247 --> 01:23:58,377
Goodness.

2086
01:23:59,617 --> 01:24:00,617
Gosh.

2087
01:24:01,247 --> 01:24:04,017
(I almost bumped into him.)

2088
01:24:05,087 --> 01:24:06,287
He's going diagonally again.

2089
01:24:06,857 --> 01:24:08,286
Gosh, he's going to bump into him.

2090
01:24:08,287 --> 01:24:09,927
I kept swimming toward the left.

2091
01:24:14,267 --> 01:24:17,097
I learned to swim from that person.

2092
01:24:17,497 --> 01:24:19,837
At a community culture center,

2093
01:24:20,067 --> 01:24:21,807
there are old ladies who are like penguins.

2094
01:24:22,167 --> 01:24:25,177
There are old women who swim like this underwater.

2095
01:24:25,277 --> 01:24:26,876
- They're really good. - There's a team of old ladies.

2096
01:24:26,877 --> 01:24:29,006
You can go straight if you keep looking at the floor...

2097
01:24:29,007 --> 01:24:30,916
because there are lines drawn.

2098
01:24:30,917 --> 01:24:33,616
- But there were no lines there. - Oh, there were no lines.

2099
01:24:33,617 --> 01:24:36,357
The ladies from the center would be upset here...

2100
01:24:36,987 --> 01:24:38,257
because there are no lines.

2101
01:24:38,517 --> 01:24:40,286
Oh, right. There are straight lines...

2102
01:24:40,287 --> 01:24:42,126
- on the swimming pool floors. - Right.

2103
01:24:42,127 --> 01:24:43,227
They didn't have one.

2104
01:24:44,357 --> 01:24:46,227
(Panting)

2105
01:24:47,027 --> 01:24:48,897
(Thinking)

2106
01:24:49,937 --> 01:24:51,967
(He suddenly dives.)

2107
01:24:52,737 --> 01:24:55,007
(Shall I try the butterfly stroke since it's a miracle morning?)

2108
01:24:56,607 --> 01:24:58,476
(Jang Woo does the standard butterfly stroke.)

2109
01:24:58,477 --> 01:25:00,547
- Jang Woo is good. - He's really good.

2110
01:25:00,807 --> 01:25:02,607
That form is really cool.

2111
01:25:04,777 --> 01:25:06,916
(Drowning)

2112
01:25:06,917 --> 01:25:08,116
Throw him a rope.

2113
01:25:08,117 --> 01:25:09,916
- Lifeguard! - Someone...

2114
01:25:09,917 --> 01:25:11,557
- Lifeguard! - Save him!

2115
01:25:12,017 --> 01:25:14,626
It was okay until the third stroke.

2116
01:25:14,627 --> 01:25:15,887
- Save him. - Someone save him.

2117
01:25:16,597 --> 01:25:18,627
You become a bit crazy.

2118
01:25:19,227 --> 01:25:20,627
You go a little crazy.

2119
01:25:22,127 --> 01:25:23,636
- You're too tensed up. - Yes.

2120
01:25:23,637 --> 01:25:25,097
Your legs are tensed up.

2121
01:25:25,897 --> 01:25:28,166
(He doesn't move forward at all.)

2122
01:25:28,167 --> 01:25:29,507
How come he's still in the same place?

2123
01:25:32,307 --> 01:25:33,406
Isn't he drowning?

2124
01:25:33,407 --> 01:25:34,407
(He struggled for a long while.)

2125
01:25:34,408 --> 01:25:35,507
That's how people swim.

2126
01:25:38,047 --> 01:25:40,487
(Flapping)

2127
01:25:42,617 --> 01:25:44,457
- You should just rest. - It must be really tiring.

2128
01:25:44,617 --> 01:25:46,086
- Yes. - I think he'll pass out...

2129
01:25:46,087 --> 01:25:47,157
during the day.

2130
01:25:52,227 --> 01:25:55,397
Does this make sense? He's swimming after running.

2131
01:25:55,827 --> 01:25:56,896
Gosh.

2132
01:25:56,897 --> 01:25:58,196
I wasn't made of iron or something.

2133
01:25:58,197 --> 01:25:59,837
It's not like you're a marine.

2134
01:26:02,307 --> 01:26:06,007
(Shaking)

2135
01:26:08,377 --> 01:26:09,616
It must be really tiring.

2136
01:26:09,617 --> 01:26:11,677
(It feels like he aged 15 years more.)

2137
01:26:12,987 --> 01:26:14,917
(It's too tiring.)

2138
01:26:17,757 --> 01:26:20,487
(He strides forward without even drying himself.)

2139
01:26:23,327 --> 01:26:25,057
(Dropping)

2140
01:26:25,957 --> 01:26:27,266
What kind of day is this?

2141
01:26:27,267 --> 01:26:29,267
(It's only 10:30am.)

2142
01:26:31,767 --> 01:26:34,736
Because I run every day, only my arms and legs are tanned.

2143
01:26:34,737 --> 01:26:35,836
My torso is pale.

2144
01:26:35,837 --> 01:26:38,277
- Right. Your torso is pale. - It was a sore sight.

2145
01:26:39,647 --> 01:26:42,677
(Lying down)

2146
01:26:44,847 --> 01:26:46,846
I can't believe someone can do that in the middle of Seoul.

2147
01:26:46,847 --> 01:26:47,917
It's so nice.

2148
01:26:49,087 --> 01:26:50,886
If I tan it for 20 minutes...

2149
01:26:50,887 --> 01:26:52,116
(I think it'll be nice if I tan it for 20 minutes.)

2150
01:26:52,117 --> 01:26:53,327
I think it'll be nice.

2151
01:26:54,927 --> 01:26:58,757
(Kian84 finally gets to rest in earnest.)

2152
01:27:00,297 --> 01:27:01,397
I should flip over.

2153
01:27:03,567 --> 01:27:07,337
(His body feels heavy.)

2154
01:27:12,137 --> 01:27:14,677
Shouldn't someone report him?

2155
01:27:14,977 --> 01:27:16,476
I saw this in "Detective Conan."

2156
01:27:16,477 --> 01:27:18,376
There's evidence.

2157
01:27:18,377 --> 01:27:19,917
Look at the side. There's a trace of water.

2158
01:27:20,087 --> 01:27:22,186
- The fact that there's water... - Isn't that a dying message?

2159
01:27:22,187 --> 01:27:23,256
It could be a dying message.

2160
01:27:23,257 --> 01:27:25,486
It is a dying message. The butt print tells something.

2161
01:27:25,487 --> 01:27:27,326
He was lying straight when he was killed,

2162
01:27:27,327 --> 01:27:29,286
but the culprit flipped him over.

2163
01:27:29,287 --> 01:27:32,227
No, it means someone with a pretty butt is the culprit.

2164
01:27:32,557 --> 01:27:35,327
(Dead men tell no tales.)

2165
01:27:35,767 --> 01:27:37,797
(The truth sinks into mystery.)

2166
01:27:40,107 --> 01:27:42,167
Oh, my. Ants are swarming around.

2167
01:27:42,967 --> 01:27:43,976
(Ants swarm around him in no time.)

2168
01:27:43,977 --> 01:27:46,106
- Why would ants swarm around? - What?

2169
01:27:46,107 --> 01:27:47,576
(Kian84 doesn't even have the energy to chase away the ants.)

2170
01:27:47,577 --> 01:27:48,577
(Groaning)

2171
01:27:49,177 --> 01:27:51,417
- Gosh, ants are swarming around. - It's a crime scene.

2172
01:27:51,917 --> 01:27:53,046
What is this?

2173
01:27:53,047 --> 01:27:54,847
(Worn out)

2174
01:27:55,787 --> 01:27:58,986
I always work out before bed,

2175
01:27:58,987 --> 01:28:00,927
so my day ended after a workout.

2176
01:28:01,087 --> 01:28:03,427
But after working out early in the morning,

2177
01:28:03,527 --> 01:28:04,927
I was given a day to myself.

2178
01:28:07,027 --> 01:28:08,167
Two.

2179
01:28:09,367 --> 01:28:11,266
(He started the day with a miracle morning.)

2180
01:28:11,267 --> 01:28:12,297
That's a long day.

2181
01:28:12,437 --> 01:28:13,966
Shouldn't he take a nap when he gets home?

2182
01:28:13,967 --> 01:28:15,037
- He should. - I should.

2183
01:28:15,367 --> 01:28:17,177
He can't help but fall asleep.

2184
01:28:17,377 --> 01:28:20,546
Those who don't run in the morning.

2185
01:28:20,547 --> 01:28:22,376
I got nine more hours to myself.

2186
01:28:22,377 --> 01:28:24,877
Running in the morning is magnificent.

2187
01:28:25,047 --> 01:28:26,516
After enduring the pain,

2188
01:28:26,517 --> 01:28:28,117
I was given nine more hours.

2189
01:28:29,687 --> 01:28:31,556
I'm exhausted.

2190
01:28:31,557 --> 01:28:32,686
You aged a little.

2191
01:28:32,687 --> 01:28:34,256
I feel like I'm dying.

2192
01:28:34,257 --> 01:28:37,257
Even with blood-shot eyes, I earned nine more hours.

2193
01:28:37,327 --> 01:28:39,967
My eyes are as red as rabbits, but I earned nine hours.

2194
01:28:40,127 --> 01:28:41,597
You can sleep after you die.

2195
01:28:41,697 --> 01:28:44,067
But you can live nine more hours with blood-shot eyes.

2196
01:28:44,237 --> 01:28:45,597
A miracle morning.

2197
01:28:46,907 --> 01:28:48,107
It must be Sean.

2198
01:28:50,177 --> 01:28:52,137
(Shaking)

2199
01:28:53,007 --> 01:28:54,977
(I'll call you later, Sean.)

2200
01:28:55,977 --> 01:28:57,176
(Gosh, these ants.)

2201
01:28:57,177 --> 01:28:58,347
Gosh, the ants.

2202
01:28:59,677 --> 01:29:01,516
(11am, Neverending Miracle)

2203
01:29:01,517 --> 01:29:02,746
What did you do afterward?

2204
01:29:02,747 --> 01:29:05,916
I went to my office and painted for two hours.

2205
01:29:05,917 --> 01:29:07,256
Then I slept afterward at home.

2206
01:29:07,257 --> 01:29:08,557
Your day became shorter.

2207
01:29:09,057 --> 01:29:10,527
Didn't your day shorten?

2208
01:29:10,797 --> 01:29:12,327
(Next story)

2209
01:29:13,427 --> 01:29:15,067
(It really is big.)

2210
01:29:15,697 --> 01:29:17,367
Who'd install this at home?

2211
01:29:19,537 --> 01:29:21,337
(What is this huge thing hanging on the wall?)

2212
01:29:22,367 --> 01:29:24,006
(The masterpiece of the century, Na Rae's broom-to-be)

2213
01:29:24,007 --> 01:29:26,207
(Reborn as a huge Naphrodite)

2214
01:29:27,377 --> 01:29:29,246
(The bigger it is, )

2215
01:29:29,247 --> 01:29:30,947
(the bigger her self-love is.)

2216
01:29:31,377 --> 01:29:32,647
(I love myself.)

2217
01:29:33,647 --> 01:29:36,586
(Another way to care for myself)

2218
01:29:36,587 --> 01:29:38,857
(Dressing up and going out)

2219
01:29:40,087 --> 01:29:41,627
(We love you, Na Rae!)

2220
01:29:42,087 --> 01:29:43,957
(My neighborhood loves me just as much as I love myself.)

2221
01:29:44,097 --> 01:29:45,627
(Where did she arrive at?)

2222
01:29:46,697 --> 01:29:49,097
(Filled with people she's glad to see)

2223
01:29:50,267 --> 01:29:52,137
(It's the bar Na Rae frequents.)

2224
01:29:53,237 --> 01:29:56,037
(Light conversations exchanged with her neighbors)

2225
01:29:56,877 --> 01:29:58,077
(They become one regardless of their nationalities.)

2226
01:29:59,307 --> 01:30:02,647
(A place where she shares her love)

2227
01:30:02,877 --> 01:30:05,246
(She carefully shares her sincerity...)

2228
01:30:05,247 --> 01:30:07,447
(by picking up the tab at a place she cherishes.)

2229
01:30:08,087 --> 01:30:10,657
(Drunk in Me, Drunk in This Place)

2230
01:30:11,487 --> 01:30:12,487
(Deficiency heat?)

2231
01:30:13,157 --> 01:30:15,087
(Me, who was skinny, had pain, and had cold hands?)

2232
01:30:15,427 --> 01:30:16,627
(Weak)

2233
01:30:17,457 --> 01:30:18,756
(I...)

2234
01:30:18,757 --> 01:30:21,267
(have changed.)

2235
01:30:21,697 --> 01:30:24,667
(Forget my past.)

2236
01:30:24,997 --> 01:30:26,337
(Charming man)

2237
01:30:27,067 --> 01:30:29,037
(A trip the charming man is taking)

2238
01:30:29,367 --> 01:30:31,477
(Jae Hyeon came to his friend's surfing shop.)

2239
01:30:31,837 --> 01:30:33,206
(His friend of 16 years...)

2240
01:30:33,207 --> 01:30:34,207
(and her husband)

2241
01:30:34,547 --> 01:30:36,177
(And their cute son, Si On)

2242
01:30:36,477 --> 01:30:38,516
(They head to the sea together to surf.)

2243
01:30:38,517 --> 01:30:40,147
(Nervous)

2244
01:30:41,017 --> 01:30:42,386
(Today's surfing instructor)

2245
01:30:42,387 --> 01:30:44,217
(Si On gives a demonstration.)

2246
01:30:44,657 --> 01:30:45,887
(Getting up)

2247
01:30:46,757 --> 01:30:47,757
(A V sign)

2248
01:30:48,527 --> 01:30:50,627
(Giving it a try, following Si On's lead)

2249
01:30:50,757 --> 01:30:52,096
I think I could do better.

2250
01:30:52,097 --> 01:30:54,127
(I'll show you my changed self.)

2251
01:30:54,867 --> 01:30:57,737
(I Will Show You My Changed Self)
